'''Alexander David Turner''' (born 6 January 1986) is an English singer, songwriter and guitarist, and the frontman of the rock band [[Arctic Monkeys]]. He is known for his [[Songwriter|lyricism]] ranging from [[kitchen sink realism]] to [[surrealism|surrealist]] wordplay, which has been praised by music critics. All but one of Turner's studio albums have topped the [[UK Albums Chart]]. He has won seven [[Brit Awards]], an [[Ivor Novello Awards|Ivor Novello Award]], and a [[Mercury Prize]] among other accolades.

When Turner was 16, he and three friends formed Arctic Monkeys. Their debut album, ''[[Whatever People Say I Am, That's What I'm Not]]'' (2006), became the fastest-selling debut album in British history and, along with the band's fifth studio album [[AM (Arctic Monkeys album)|''AM'']] (2013), appeared on [[Rolling Stone's 500 Greatest Albums of All Time|''Rolling Stone''<nowiki/>'s 500 Greatest Albums of All Time]] and other lists. The band has experimented with [[Palm Desert Scene|desert rock]], indie pop, R&B, and [[lounge music]]. He also co-founded [[The Last Shadow Puppets]] with [[Miles Kane]] in 2007, who have released two [[orchestral pop]] albums: ''[[The Age of the Understatement]]'' (2008) and ''[[Everything You've Come to Expect]]'' (2016).

Turner provided [[Submarine (EP)|an acoustic soundtrack]] for the feature film ''[[Submarine (2010 film)|Submarine]]'' (2010), which additionally served as his solo debut [[extended play]] (EP). He co-wrote and co-produced [[Alexandra Savior]]'s debut album ''[[Belladonna of Sadness (album)|Belladonna of Sadness]]'' (2017).

==Early life==
Alexander David Turner was born in [[Sheffield]] on 6 January 1986,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.upi.com/Top_News/2019/01/06/UPI-Almanac-for-Sunday-Jan-6-2019/8591546568650/|title=UPI Almanac for Sunday, Jan. 6, 2019|work=[[United Press International]]|date=6 January 2019|access-date=10 September 2019}}</ref><ref name="GRO birth">{{cite web|title=Alexander David Turner in the England & Wales, Civil Registration Birth Index, 1916–2007|url=https://search.ancestry.co.uk/cgi-bin/sse.dll?_phsrc=SWL1&_phstart=successSource&usePUBJs=true&indiv=1&dbid=8782&gsfn=Alexander%20David&gsln=Turner&cp=0&msmns=Turner&msbdy=1986&msbdm=1&msbpn__ftp=sheffield,%20yorkshire,%20england,%20united%20kingdom&msbpn=1652386&new=1&rank=1&uidh=2m1&redir=false&msT=1&gss=angs-d&pcat=34&fh=0&h=10472327&recoff=&ml_rpos=1&queryId=b967456c445206e545f4f7fa0ca1b84c|publisher=[[Ancestry.com]]}}</ref> the only child of secondary school teachers Penny (''née'' Druce)<ref name="GRO birth"/> and David Turner.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sterdan |first1=Darryl |title=Monkeys Still Shining for Turner|url=http://www.torontosun.com/2011/09/27/monkeys-still-shining-for-turner |access-date=24 July 2018 |newspaper=[[Toronto Sun]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151125134546/http://www.torontosun.com/2011/09/27/monkeys-still-shining-for-turner |archive-date=25 November 2015}}</ref> He was raised in Sheffield's [[High Green]] suburb.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nme.com/reviews/arctic-monkeys-receive-heros-welcome-dazzle-sheffield-huge-homecoming-show-2380865|title=The Arctic Monkeys from High Green, Sheffield make their jubilant return home|date=19 September 2018|work=NME|access-date=18 April 2020 }}</ref> He has said that his parents came from "very different backgrounds"; his mother, from [[Amersham|Amersham, Buckinghamshire]],<ref name=poetscornered>{{cite news |last1=Gilbert |first1=Pat |title=Poets Cornered |work=MOJO Magazine |date=2007 |page=46}}</ref> taught German and was "fascinated by language".<ref name="NME 2018">{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys talk to NME in 2007 about 'Favourite Worst Nightmare' |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/arctic-monkeys-nme-interview-2007-favourite-worst-nightmare-2307701 |access-date=15 January 2020 |work=NME |date=8 May 2018}}</ref> His father, a Sheffield native,<ref name=poetscornered/> taught music and physics.<ref name="day">{{cite news |last1=Day |first1=Elizabeth |title=Arctic Monkeys: 'In Mexico it was like Beatlemania' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2013/oct/27/arctic-monkeys-mexico-beatlemania |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=26 October 2013 }}</ref> Turner's parents were both music fans and his earliest musical memories involve [[the Beatles]]<ref name="americansongwriter.com"/><ref name="QMag07">{{cite magazine |title=The Reluctant Genius of Alex Turner |first=Keith |last=Cameron |magazine=[[Q (magazine)|Q]] |number=252 |date=July 2007}}</ref> and [[the Beach Boys]].<ref name="stereogum.com">{{cite web |url=https://www.stereogum.com/715661/progress-report-arctic-monkeys/franchises/progress-report/ |title=Progress Report: Arctic Monkeys |first=Rachel&nbsp;T. |last=Cole|date=31 May 2011 |website=[[Stereogum]] |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref> During car journeys, his mother played music by [[Led Zeppelin]],<ref name="hiatt">{{cite magazine |last1=Hiatt |first1=Brian |title=How Arctic Monkeys Reinvented Their Sound |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/how-arctic-monkeys-reinvented-their-sound-85214/ |access-date=27 July 2018 |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=21 October 2013}}</ref> [[David Bowie]],<ref name="absolute"/> and [[the Eagles]].<ref name="pitchfork.com">{{cite web |url=https://pitchfork.com/features/5-10-15-20/8832-alex-turner/ |title=5–10–15–20: Alex Turner |first=Ryan |last=Dombal |date=10 May 2012 |website=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]] |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref> His father was a fan of jazz and [[swing music]],<ref name="absolute">{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys interview on Absolute Radio |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MkG3TwRgbSM  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/MkG3TwRgbSM |archive-date=21 December 2021 |url-status=live|access-date=24 July 2018 |work=[[Absolute Radio]]'s Youtube |date=7 November 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref> particularly [[Frank Sinatra]],<ref name="americansongwriter.com">{{cite news |last1=Matheny |first1=Skip |title=Drinks With: Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.americansongwriter.com/2010/03/drinks-with-arctic-monkeys/ |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=[[American Songwriter]] |date=12 March 2010}}</ref> and had played the saxophone, trumpet, and piano in [[big band]]s.<ref name="QMag07"/><ref name="Sydney">{{cite news |url=https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/music/monkey-business-the-evolution-of-arctic-monkeys-20180503-h0zkze.html |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner on long-awaited album Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino |first=Barry |last=Divola |date=11 May 2018 |newspaper=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date=15 May 2018}}</ref> Turner himself was taught some scales on the family keyboard by his father<ref name="Sydney"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys Alex Turner BBC Radio 1 Interview – 10th May 2018 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7EWtXOf44v0&t=71s  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/7EWtXOf44v0 |archive-date=21 December 2021 |url-status=live|access-date=5 February 2020 }}{{cbignore}}</ref> and took professional piano lessons until he was eight years old.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/jul/12/arctic-monkeys-interviewed-simon-armitage |title=Highly evolved |first=Simon |last=Armitage |newspaper=[[The Observer]] |date=12 July 2009 |access-date=28 March 2016}}</ref><ref name="americansongwriter.com"/>

From the age of five, Turner grew up alongside neighbour [[Matt Helders]]; they attended primary school, secondary school, and college together.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Halo |first1=Martin |title=Interview: Arctic Monkeys 'Setting Fire to the Dancefloor'  |url=https://www.thewaster.com/interview/arctic-monkeys-setting-fire-to-the-dancefloor/ |access-date=12 August 2018 |work=TheWaster.com |date=30 May 2007}}</ref><ref name="jill_helders">{{cite news |title=Jill Helders: Arctic Mummy |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/southyorkshire/content/articles/2009/02/25/women_behind_the_men_jill_feature.shtml |access-date=25 July 2018 |date= 25 February 2009 |work=[[BBC Yorkshire]] }}</ref> At their primary school graduation ceremony, Turner and Helders joined some other friends in a mimed performance of [[Oasis (band)|Oasis]]' "[[Morning Glory (Oasis song)|Morning Glory]]"{{emdash}}Helders played the role of [[Liam Gallagher]] while Turner pretended to play the bass guitar, using a tennis racket as his instrument.<ref name="pitchfork.com"/><ref name="Welcome to the jungle">{{cite news |title=Welcome to the jungle |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2006-apr-02-ca-artic2-story.html |access-date=26 January 2020 |work=Los Angeles Times |date=2 April 2006}}</ref> The two met [[Andy Nicholson]] in secondary school,<ref name=thawed>{{cite news |last1=McLean |first1=Craig |title=Craig McLean spends three months on the road with the Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/jan/01/popandrock.arcticmonkeys |access-date=26 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=1 January 2006 }}</ref> and the three friends bonded over their shared enjoyment of hip-hop artists such as [[Dr. Dre]],<ref name=laurenmurphy>{{cite news |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/culture/music/arctic-monkeys-get-funky-on-album-number-five-1.1517333 |title=Arctic Monkeys get funky on album number five |first=Lauren |last=Murphy |date=5 September 2013 |newspaper=[[The Irish Times]] |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref> the [[Wu-Tang Clan]],<ref name="pitchfork.com"/> [[Outkast]],<ref name="pitchfork.com"/> and [[Roots Manuva]].<ref name="pitchfork.com"/> They spent their time playing basketball, skateboarding, riding [[BMX]]s,<ref name=viahillsborough/> and "making crap hip-hop" beats using Turner's father's [[Steinberg Cubase|Cubase]] system.<ref name="orbiterlover.com">{{cite web |url=https://orbiterlover.com/2015/01/29/andy-nicholson-arctic-monkey-sticky-blood/ |title=Hanging Out With Andy Nicholson: Ex-Arctic Monkey, Producer & Photographer |date=29 January 2015 |website=Orbiter Lover |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref><ref name="QMag07"/> Turner and his friends became interested in rock music following the breakthrough of [[the Strokes]] in 2001.<ref name="Los Angeles Times">{{cite news |url=https://articles.latimes.com/2006/apr/02/entertainment/ca-artic2/3 |title=Welcome to the jungle |newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]] |access-date=26 August 2015 |first=Robert |last=Hilburn |date=2 April 2006 |archive-date=29 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150929024911/http://articles.latimes.com/2006/apr/02/entertainment/ca-artic2/3 |url-status=dead }}</ref> His father let him borrow a school guitar to learn a "couple of chords" when Turner was 15<ref name="QMag07"/> and, for Christmas that year, his parents bought him an electric guitar.<ref name="Caesar">{{cite news |last=Caesar |first=Ed |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/alex-turner-thats-what-hes-not-so-what-is-he-444611.html |newspaper=[[The Independent]] |title=Alex Turner: That's what he's not. So what is he? |date=14 April 2007 |access-date=17 February 2013 |location=London |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110517160144/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/alex-turner-thats-what-hes-not-so-what-is-he-444611.html |archive-date=17 May 2011}}</ref>

Turner was educated at [[Stocksbridge High School]] from 1997 to 2002. His former teacher, Mark Coleman, characterised him as a "bright" and "popular" student who excelled at sports rather than music.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coleman |first1=Mark |title=What were today's celebrities like as children? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2008/sep/13/celebrity.television |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=12 September 2008 }}</ref> His English and drama teacher, Steve Baker, remembered him as a clever pupil who was "quite reserved" and "a little bit different".<ref name="schoolteachers"/> He noted that Turner had an "incredibly laid-back" approach to his studies, which worried his mother and led to criticism from other teachers.<ref name="schoolteachers"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Baker |first1=Simon |title=Human Potential and the Arctic Monkeys. – Steve Baker Education |url=http://www.stevebakereducation.co.uk/human-potential-and-the-arctic-monkeys/ |access-date=21 January 2020 |work=www.stevebakereducation.co.uk |archive-date=4 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304081923/http://www.stevebakereducation.co.uk/human-potential-and-the-arctic-monkeys/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> While there were books at home,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Armitage |first1=Simon |title=Simon Armitage meets Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/jul/12/arctic-monkeys-interviewed-simon-armitage |work=The Observer |date=11 July 2009|access-date=15 September 2015}}</ref> Turner did not read regularly<ref>{{cite news |last1=Button |first1=James |title=Back for Seconds |url=https://www.theage.com.au/entertainment/music/back-for-seconds-20070415-ge4ngu.html |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=[[The Age]] |date=15 April 2007 }}</ref> and was too self-conscious to share his writing with others.<ref name="rickmartin">{{cite news |last1=Martin |first1=Rick |title=Arctic Monkeys: Read Their First Ever NME Feature – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/archive-arctic-monkeys-read-their-first-ever-nme-feature |access-date=25 May 2005 |work=[[NME]] |date=5 April 2011}}</ref> Nonetheless, he enjoyed English lessons.<ref name="schoolteachers">{{cite news |last1=Rogers |first1=Jude |title=Schoolteachers of Rock |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2010/apr/25/inspirational-music-teachers |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=25 April 2010 }}</ref> Turner then attended [[Barnsley College]] from 2002 to 2004. Given the opportunity to "get away without doing maths", he largely opted out of the "substantial" subjects required for university entry.<ref name="QMag07"/> He studied for [[GCE Advanced Level|A-levels]] in music technology and media studies, as well as AS-levels in English, photography, and psychology.<ref name=college>{{cite news |last1=Shimmon |first1=Katie |title=College days |url=https://www.theguardian.com/education/2005/nov/29/furthereducation.uk1 |access-date=18 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=29 November 2005 }}</ref>

== Career ==

===Arctic Monkeys===

==== Early years and foundation ====
At the age of 15, Turner's weekends revolved around girls and drinking cider with his friends.<ref name="pitchfork">{{cite web |last1=Plagenhoef |first1=Scott |title=Scott Walker 'Til the Band Comes In |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/12090-til-the-band-comes-in/ |date=11 August 2008 |website=[[Pitchfork Media]] |access-date=15 July 2016}}</ref> [[Joe Carnall]], a schoolfriend, has said Turner was "always the quiet one" in their social circle.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moody |first1=Paul |title=It's great when you're ape..yeah! |work=Uncut Magazine |date=December 2006 |page=74}}</ref> After friends began forming bands and playing live,<ref name="theguardian 2006-04">{{cite news |author=<!--Not stated-->|title=Weekend: The Arctic Monkeys in New York |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/apr/15/popandrock.arcticmonkeys |access-date=23 January 2020 |work=The Guardian |date=15 April 2006}}</ref> Turner, Helders, and Nicholson decided to start Arctic Monkeys in mid-2002.<ref name=pitchfork/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Simpson |first1=Dave |title=Made of steel: how South Yorkshire became the British indie heartland |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2018/feb/02/made-of-steel-how-south-yorkshire-became-the-british-indie-heartland |access-date=25 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=2 February 2018 }}</ref> According to Nicholson, Turner already had "instruments about the house" and was conversant in the basics of musicianship because of his father's job as a music teacher.<ref name=viahillsborough>{{cite news |title=Episode 53: Via Hillsborough, Please (ft Andy Nicholson) |url=https://soundcloud.com/user-722232045/episode-53-via-hillsborough-please-ft-andy-nicholson |access-date=5 January 2020 }}</ref> Helders bought a drum kit,<ref name="loudandquiet">{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys' Matt Helders at 16 – he left school and spent his savings on a drum kit stored in Alex Turner's garage |url=https://www.loudandquiet.com/short/arctic-monkeys-matt-helders-at-16-he-left-school-and-spent-his-savings-on-a-drum-kit-stored-in-alex-turners-garage/ |access-date=5 January 2020 |work=Loud And Quiet}}</ref> while Turner suggested that Nicholson learn bass guitar, and invited [[Arctic Monkeys|Jamie Cook]], a neighbour who attended a different school, to play guitar.<ref name=viahillsborough/> Initially, Turner played guitar in the instrumental band; he became the frontman when two other school friends declined to sing.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stitcher.com/podcast/anchor-podcasts/the-279-podcast/e/62942273|title=Andy Nicholson {{!}} The 279 Podcast |date=1 August 2019|access-date=5 January 2020}}</ref> Helders considered Turner the obvious candidate for lyricist – "I knew he had a thing for words" – and he gradually began to share songs with his bandmates.<ref name=":Sunday Times">{{cite web |last=Murison |first=Krissi |date=20 May 2018 |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner on their new album and returning to the limelight|url=https://www.thetimes.com/article/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-on-their-new-album-and-returning-to-the-limelight-zm6f5h5zs|url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20200224151815/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-on-their-new-album-and-returning-to-the-limelight-zm6f5h5zs|archive-date=24 February 2020 |access-date=30 March 2023 |website=[[The Sunday Times]]}}</ref><ref name="thenational.ae">{{cite news |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/music/monkey-business-1.509641/ |last1=Odell |first1=Michael |title=Monkey business |date=15 August 2009 |newspaper=[[The National (Abu Dhabi)|The National]] |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref> Before playing a live show, the band rehearsed for a year in Turner's garage<ref name="loudandquiet" /> and, later, at an unused warehouse in [[Wath-upon-Dearne|Wath]].<ref name="jill_helders"/> According to Helders' mother, who drove the teenagers to and from their rehearsal space three times a week: "If they knew you were there, they would just stop so we had to sneak in."<ref name="jill_helders"/> Their first gig was on Friday, 13 June 2003, supporting The Sound at a local pub called The Grapes.<ref name="thetimes.co.uk">{{cite news |url=https://www.thetimes.com/sunday-times-rich-list/profile/article/arctic-monkeys-hit-their-stride-gnhszmdw6qz |title=Arctic Monkeys hit their stride |first=Craig |last=McLean |date=21 November 2009 |newspaper=The Times |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref> The set, which was partly recorded,<ref>{{cite news |title=The real story about the Arctic Monkeys first ever gig at The Grapes in Sheffield. – Reyt Good Magazine |url=https://www.rgm.press/the-real-story-about-the-arctic-monkeys-fist-ever-gig-at-the-grapes-in-sheffield/ |access-date=16 July 2018 |work=www.rgm.press}}</ref> comprised four original songs and four cover versions of songs by [[the Beatles]], [[the White Stripes]], [[the Undertones]], and [[the Datsuns]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys, Geese Confession, Job Interview Hacks, Jo Whiley & Simon Mayo – BBC Radio 2 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b0b6pdfn |access-date=12 July 2018 |work=BBC}}</ref><ref name=thawed/>

In the summer of 2003, Turner played seven gigs in York and Liverpool as a rhythm guitarist for the funk band Judan Suki, after meeting the lead singer [[Jon McClure]] on a bus.<ref name="NME 2018"/><ref name="stokes">{{cite news |last1=Stokes |first1=Paul |title=Alex Turner |work=Q Magazine |issue=November 2011 |page=63}}</ref> That August, while recording a demo with Judan Suki at Sheffield's 2fly Studios, Turner asked [[Alan Smyth]] if he would produce an Arctic Monkeys demo. Smyth obliged and "thought they definitely had something special going on. I told Alex off for singing in an American voice at that first session."<ref name="Blender06">{{cite magazine |title=The Lads Are Alright |first=Dorian |last=Lynskey |magazine=[[Blender (magazine)|Blender]] |date=May 2006}}</ref> An introduction by Smyth led to the band acquiring a management team, Geoff Barradale and Ian McAndrew.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.counterfeitmag.co.uk/features/alan-smyth/ |title=Alan Smyth |first=Chris |last=Saunders |work=Counterfeit Magazine |access-date=26 August 2015|date=17 October 2011 }}</ref> They paid for Smyth and Arctic Monkeys to record numerous three-song demos in 2003 and 2004.<ref>{{cite news |title=Monkey business: Ian McAndrew on 25 years of Wildlife Entertainment – Music Business Worldwide |url=https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/monkey-business-ian-mcandrew-on-25-years-of-wildlife-entertainment/ |access-date=21 July 2018 |work=Music Business Worldwide |date=31 July 2015}}</ref> Turner was quiet and observant during studio sessions, remembered Smyth: "Whenever anyone popped in the studio, he would sit and listen to them before he would say anything."<ref name="Blender06"/> At their rehearsal room in [[Yellow Arch Studios]], Arctic Monkeys developed a reputation as particularly hard workers; the owner lent the band touring equipment while the owner's wife helped Turner with his singing.<ref>{{cite news |title=Inside the room where Arctic Monkeys first rehearsed in Sheffield: 'They were definitely going to be stars'|url=https://www.thestar.co.uk/news/inside-room-where-arctic-monkeys-first-rehearsed-sheffield-they-were-definitely-going-be-stars-469568 |access-date=22 January 2020 |work=www.thestar.co.uk }}</ref> Barradale drove the band around venues in Scotland, the Midlands, and the north of England to establish their reputation as a live band.<ref name=thawed/> The band handed out free copies of the demo CDs after each show<ref name="Monkey business">{{cite news |title=Monkey business |url=https://www.independent.ie/entertainment/monkey-business-26736842.html |access-date=15 January 2020 |work=Independent.ie }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Boyd |first1=Brian |title=Primates of all England |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/culture/primates-of-all-england-1.1202742 |access-date=20 January 2020 |newspaper=The Irish Times |date=20 April 2007 }}</ref> and fans began sharing the unofficial ''[[Beneath the Boardwalk]]'' demo compilation online.<ref name=sfgate>{{cite news |last1=Lanham |first1=Tom |title=Monkey business |url=https://www.sfgate.com/entertainment/article/Monkey-business-2520547.php |access-date=26 July 2018 |work=SFGate |date=2 April 2006}}</ref>

After finishing college in mid-2004, Turner took a year out to focus on the band<ref name="college" /> and deferred vague plans to attend university in Manchester.<ref name="QMag07" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Go Nuts with the Monkeys |url=https://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/news/showbiz-news/go-nuts-with-the-monkeys-999918 |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=[[Manchester Evening News]] |date=29 August 2007}}</ref> He began working part-time as a bartender at the Sheffield music venue [[Boardwalk (nightclub)|The Boardwalk]]. There, he met well-known figures including musician [[Richard Hawley]] and poet [[John Cooper Clarke]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Creighton |first1=Keith |title=Richard Hawley Aims His Guitar At The 1 Percent |url=https://popdose.com/richard-hawley-aims-his-guitar-at-the-1-percent/ |access-date=11 August 2018 |work=Popdose |date=21 August 2012}}</ref><ref name="Pitchfork-09-13">{{cite web |url=https://pitchfork.com/features/interviews/9221-arctic-monkeys/ |title=Interviews: Arctic Monkeys |last=Fitzmaurice |first=Larry |date=19 September 2013 |website=Pitchfork |access-date=28 March 2016 |archive-date=10 November 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151110085817/http://pitchfork.com/features/interviews/9221-arctic-monkeys/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> By the end of 2004, Arctic Monkeys' audiences were beginning to sing along with their songs<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hasted |first1=Nick |title=Year of the Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/features/year-of-the-arctic-monkeys-519609.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140307150140/http://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/features/year-of-the-arctic-monkeys-519609.html |archive-date=7 March 2014 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |access-date=30 July 2018 |work=The Independent |date=16 December 2005}}</ref> and the demo of "[[I Bet You Look Good on The Dancefloor]]" (then called ''Bet You Look Good on The Dancefloor'', as it was then the ''[[Beneath the Boardwalk]]'' version) was played on [[BBC Radio 1]] by [[Zane Lowe]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/southyorkshire/content/articles/2005/02/04/arctic_monkeys_feature.shtml |title=Too much Monkey business? |first=Rob |last=Webb |date=4 February 2005 |website=BBC South Yorkshire |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref>

==== National rise to prominence ====
[[File:Alex Turner Arctic Monkeys live (cropped).jpg|alt=|left|thumb|Turner performing in Newcastle Academy, 2006|200x200px]]
Arctic Monkeys came to national attention in early 2005. They received their first mention in a national newspaper in April, with a ''Daily Star'' reporter describing them as "the most exciting band to emerge this year".<ref>{{cite news |title=Insiders' guide to Arctic Monkeys |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/6567861.stm |access-date=29 July 2018 |work=BBC |date=23 April 2007}}</ref> They self-released [[Five Minutes with Arctic Monkeys|an EP]], featuring the single "[[Fake Tales of San Francisco]]", in May<ref>{{cite news |last1=Webb |first1=Rob |title=Single Review: Arctic Monkeys – Five Minutes With Arctic Monkeys |url=http://drownedinsound.com/releases/6595/reviews/12298- |access-date=29 July 2018 |work=DrownedInSound |date=25 May 2005 |archive-date=19 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180719024618/http://drownedinsound.com/releases/6595/reviews/12298- |url-status=dead }}</ref> and commenced their first nationwide tour soon afterwards.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hey, hey it's the Monkeys! |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/southyorkshire/content/articles/2005/10/19/arctic_monkeys_interview.shtml |publisher=BBC Yorkshire |access-date=27 July 2018 |date=2005}}</ref> In June, in the midst of a bidding war, Arctic Monkeys signed to the independent label [[Domino Recording Company]].<ref name=exlaim>{{cite web |last1=Muir |first1=Ava |date=21 June 2018 |title=Arctic Monkeys' Career So Far: From Rubble to the Ritz |url=https://exclaim.ca/music/article/arctic_monkeys_career_retrospective_from_rubble_to_the_ritz |access-date=27 July 2018 |website=[[Exclaim!]]}}</ref> After initial sessions with [[James Ford (musician)|James Ford]] and [[Mike Crossey]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys – How We Wrote 'I Bet You Look Good On The Dancefloor' |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kfwa6_72GeY&t=26s  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/kfwa6_72GeY |archive-date=21 December 2021 |url-status=live|access-date=24 January 2020 }}{{cbignore}}</ref> they recorded an album in rural [[Lincolnshire]] with producer [[Jim Abbiss]].<ref name=exlaim/> In October, the single "[[I Bet You Look Good on the Dancefloor]]" debuted at number one on the UK Singles Chart.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Barton |first1=Laura |title=The question: Have the Arctic Monkeys changed the music business? |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2005/oct/25/popandrock.arcticmonkeys |access-date=29 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=25 October 2005 }}</ref> ''[[Whatever People Say I Am, That's What I'm Not]]'', Arctic Monkeys' debut album, was released in January 2006, and debuted at number one on the [[UK Albums Chart]]. Turner's lyrics, chronicling teenage nightlife in Sheffield, were widely praised.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sullivan |first1=Caroline |title=Caroline Sullivan: Whatever they say they are, that's what they are |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/jan/27/popandrock.arcticmonkeys |access-date=21 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=27 January 2006 }}</ref> [[Kelefa Sanneh]] of ''The New York Times'' remarked: "Mr. Turner's lyrics are worth waiting for and often worth memorizing, too ... He has an uncanny way of evoking Northern English youth culture while neither romanticizing it nor sneering at it."<ref name=teenspirit>{{cite news |last1=Sanneh |first1=Kelefa |author-link1=Kelefa Sanneh |title=Teen Spirit: Arctic Monkeys Observed in the Wild |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/01/30/arts/music/30monkeys.html?pagewanted=all |website=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=27 July 2018 |date=30 January 2006}}</ref> Musically, [[Alexis Petridis]] of ''The Guardian'' noted that the album was influenced by guitar bands "from the past five years ... Thrillingly, their music doesn't sound apologetic for not knowing the intricacies of rock history."<ref name="petridis1">{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |title=CD: Arctic Monkeys, Whatever People Say I Am, That's What I'm Not |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/jan/13/popandrock.shopping6 |access-date=20 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=13 January 2006 }}</ref>

It was the fastest-selling debut album in British music history and quickly became a cultural phenomenon.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cromelin |first1=Richard |title=Arctic Monkeys play it cool |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2006-mar-15-et-monkeys15-story.html |access-date=21 July 2018 |work=Los Angeles Times |date=15 March 2006}}</ref> Turner was hailed by British press outlets as "the voice of a generation".<ref name=teenspirit/> In interview profiles, however, he was described as quiet and uncomfortable with attention.<ref name="theguardian 2006-04"/> The band dismissed the hype, with Cook saying their goal was "to be able to grow like [[The Clash]]. When they started, it was a very basic, punky record. Then they started to take off and move in lots of directions. That's what we want."<ref name="Welcome to the jungle"/> Less than two months after the album's release, Turner declared that Sheffield-inspired songwriting was "a closed book": "We're moving on and thinking about different things."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bendersky |first1=Ari |title=Monkeys of the Arctic Kind |url=https://tucson.com/entertainment/music/monkeys-of-the-arctic-kind/article_fcbb30bb-3de5-5f1d-9525-a58a7c0b9186.html |access-date=30 July 2018 |work=Arizona Daily Star |date=1 March 2006 }}</ref> Years later, Turner said that the attention during this period made him "a bit frightened or nervous":<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kandell |first1=Steve |title=How to Suddenly Become the Biggest Band Ever |work=Spin Magazine |date=June 2007 |page=79}}</ref> "We shut a lot of people out, just to try to keep some sort of control."<ref name="Barbara theguardian">{{cite web |last=Ellen |first=Barbara |date=29 May 2011 |title=Arctic Monkeys: 'We want to get better rather than get bigger'|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2011/may/29/arctic-monkeys-interview-barbara-ellen|url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230607060040/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2011/may/29/arctic-monkeys-interview-barbara-ellen|archive-date=7 June 2023 |access-date=24 August 2023 |website=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> The band turned down many promotional opportunities<ref name="independent hasted">{{cite news |last1=Hasted |first1=Nick |title=Arctic Monkeys: Too much monkey business |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/arctic-monkeys-too-much-monkey-business-479704.html |access-date=13 January 2020 |work=The Independent |date=26 May 2006 }}</ref> and quickly released new material – a five-track EP ''[[Who the Fuck Are Arctic Monkeys?]]'' in April, and a stand-alone single, "[[Leave Before the Lights Come On]]", in August. That summer, the band made the decision to permanently replace Nicholson,<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys bass player quits |url=https://www.smh.com.au/entertainment/music/arctic-monkeys-bass-player-quits-20060621-gdnsuc.html |access-date=30 July 2018 |work=The Sydney Morning Herald |date=21 June 2006 }}</ref> who had taken a touring break due to "fatigue",<ref name="independent hasted"/> with [[Nick O'Malley]], another childhood friend.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Raihala |first1=Ross |title=Better late than never for Arctic Monkeys' bassist |url=https://www.twincities.com/2007/05/05/better-late-than-never-for-arctic-monkeys-bassist/ |access-date=30 July 2018 |work=[[St. Paul Pioneer Press]] |date=5 May 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=says |first1=Chris |title=Arctic Monkeys interview : Illinois Entertainer |url=https://illinoisentertainer.com/2009/12/arctic-monkeys-interview/|website=Illinoisentertasiner.com |access-date=12 January 2020}}</ref> Nicholson was informed at a band meeting, during which "Al did the speaking."<ref name=viahillsborough/> Turner and Nicholson stopped speaking for two years<ref>{{cite news |title=Ex Arctic Monkeys bassist considered suicide after leaving band |url=https://www.radiox.co.uk/artists/arctic-monkeys/ex-bassist-andy-nicholson-considered-suicide-after/ |access-date=13 January 2020 |work=Radio X }}</ref> but later repaired their friendship.<ref>{{cite news |title=Former Arctic Monkeys bassist Andy Nicholson tells us about life after the band and going solo as Goldteeth |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/former-arctic-monkeys-bassist-andy-nicholson-tells-us-about-life-after-the-band-and-going-solo-as-goldteeth-2508404 |access-date=13 January 2020 |work=NME |date=14 June 2019}}</ref><ref name="orbiterlover.com"/>

[[File:Arctic-Monkeys-singer.jpg|thumb|Turner performing at [[Coachella Valley Music and Arts Festival|Coachella]] in May 2007|225x225px]]
Arctic Monkeys' second album, ''[[Favourite Worst Nightmare]]'', was released in April 2007, just over a year after their debut.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cheeky Monkeys do it their way |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/music/rockandjazzmusic/3649807/Cheeky-Monkeys-do-it-their-way.html |access-date=6 February 2020 |date=2 February 2006}}</ref> It was produced in London by [[Mike Crossey]] and [[James Ford (musician)|James Ford]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Robinson |first1=John |title=Alex Turner Q & A – Uncut |url=https://www.uncut.co.uk/features/alex-turner-q-a-42884 |access-date=1 August 2018 |work=Uncut |date=23 April 2007}}</ref> As of 2020, Ford has produced every subsequent Turner project.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys co-producer James Ford on new album 'AM' {{!}} NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/arctic-monkeys-co-producer-james-ford-on-new-album-am-767042 |access-date=28 January 2020 |work=NME |date=1 August 2013}}</ref> Lyrically, the album touches on fame, love, and heartache.<ref name="marchogan">{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Marc |title=Arctic Monkeys: Favourite Worst Nightmare Album Review |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/10150-favourite-worst-nightmare/ |access-date=2 August 2018 |work=Pitchfork |date=24 April 2007 }}</ref> Johanna Bennett, Turner's then girlfriend, was credited as a co-writer on "[[Fluorescent Adolescent]]".<ref name=":2">{{cite web|url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/3am/celebrity-news/arctic-heartache-446764|title=ARCTIC HEARTACHE|date=15 January 2007|work=The Mirror|access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{cite news | url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2007/jul/15/musicnews.music | title=Ex-girlfriend helps Arctic Monkeys to a hit | first=Jaimie | last=Hodgson | work=[[The Observer]] | publisher=Guardian News and Media | date=15 July 2007 | access-date=23 August 2008 | location=London| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080726221602/http://www.guardian.co.uk/uk/2007/jul/15/musicnews.music| archive-date= 26 July 2008 | url-status= live}}</ref> While uninterested in the songs concerning fame, Marc Hogan of ''Pitchfork'' said the album displayed Turner's "usual gift for vivid imagery" and explored "new emotional depth".<ref name="marchogan"/> Petridis of ''The Guardian'' noted that the band were "pushing gently but confidently at the boundaries of their sound", with hints of "woozy psychedelia" and "piledriving metal".<ref name=petridis2>{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |title=CD: Arctic Monkeys, Favourite Worst Nightmare |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2007/apr/20/popandrock.alexispetridis |access-date=22 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=20 April 2007 }}</ref> The album was a commercial success, debuting at number one in the UK,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Swash |first1=Rosie |title=Arctic Monkeys outselling the rest of the top 20 combined |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2007/apr/25/news.arcticmonkeys |access-date=2 August 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=25 April 2007 }}</ref> while Arctic Monkeys headlined [[Glastonbury Festival]] in the summer of 2007.<ref name="swash">{{cite news |last1=Swash |first1=Rosie |title=Review: Arctic Monkeys, Pyramid stage, Friday 11.05pm |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2007/jun/23/glastonbury2007.glastonbury5 |access-date=22 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=23 June 2007 }}</ref> Also that year, Turner began to collaborate with other artists. He worked with rapper [[Dizzee Rascal]] on the Arctic Monkeys B-side "[[Brianstorm|Temptation]]", a version of which also featured on Rascal's album ''[[Maths and English]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Thompson |first1=Ben |title=Ben Thompson speaks frankly to Dizzie Rascal |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2007/apr/22/features.musicmonthly5 |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=21 April 2007 }}</ref> He co-wrote three songs on [[Reverend and The Makers]]' debut album ''[[The State of Things (album)|The State Of Things]]'', after briefly sharing a Sheffield flat with the frontman Jon McClure.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Youngs |first1=Ian |title=Reverend preaches the power of pop |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/entertainment/7034076.stm |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=BBC |date=9 October 2007}}</ref> Another Sheffield singer, [[Richard Hawley]], featured on the Arctic Monkeys' B-side "[[Teddy Picker|Bad Woman]]" and performed with the band at the [[O2 Apollo Manchester|Manchester Apollo]], as part of a [[At the Apollo|concert film]] directed by [[Richard Ayoade]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/southyorkshire/content/articles/2008/01/17/richard_hawley_interview.shtml |title=Interview: Richard Hawley |date=25 November 2008 |website=BBC South Yorkshire |access-date=28 March 2016}}</ref>

Turner has described Arctic Monkeys' third album, ''[[Humbug (album)|Humbug]]'', released in August 2009, as "a massive turning point" in the band's career.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/music/rockandpopmusic/10478485/Arctic-Monkeys-QandA.html |title=Arctic Monkeys Q&A |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |date=27 November 2013 |access-date=28 March 2016}}</ref> They travelled to [[Joshua Tree, California]] to work with producer [[Josh Homme]] of [[Queens of the Stone Age]]; it was the band's first experience of working in a studio for an extended period of time.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Eliscu|first=Jenny |title=Arctic Monkeys' Desert Journey: The Making of 'Humbug' |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/arctic-monkeys-desert-journey-the-making-of-humbug-69144/ |access-date=13 February 2020 |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=3 September 2009}}</ref> Homme has said the album's heavier sound was initiated by the band themselves, while he encouraged Turner to embrace longer guitar solos<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.uncut.co.uk/features/arctic-monkey-alex-turner-gives-uncut-the-lowdown-on-new-lp-37345 |title=Arctic Monkey Alex Turner gives Uncut the lowdown on new LP! |magazine=Uncut |date=3 August 2009 |access-date=28 March 2016}}</ref> and to develop his newfound "crooning" style of singing.<ref>{{cite news |title=Josh Homme |url=https://www.soundonsound.com/people/josh-homme |access-date=13 February 2020 |work=www.soundonsound.com}}</ref> While Petridis of ''The Guardian'' found some lyrics "too oblique to connect", he was impressed by the band's "desire to progress". He described "[[Cornerstone (song)|Cornerstone]]" as a "dazzling display of what Turner can do: a fabulously witty, poignant evocation of lost love."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |title=Arctic Monkeys: Humbug {{!}} CD review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/aug/20/arctic-monkeys-humbug-cd-review |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=20 August 2009 }}</ref> Joe Tangari of ''Pitchfork'' felt the album was a "legitimate expansion of the band's songwriting arsenal" and described "Cornerstone" as the highlight.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys: Humbug Album Review {{!}} Pitchfork |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/13374-humbug/ |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com }}</ref> During a break in the UK ''Humbug'' tour, Turner joined Richard Hawley on stage at a London charity concert,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Rodrigo |title=Hawley joined by Monkey on stage |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/entertainment/8370337.stm |access-date=11 August 2018 |work=BBC News |date=20 November 2009}}</ref> and played a seven-song acoustic set.<ref name="Empire guardian">{{cite news |last1=Empire |first1=Kitty |title=Richard Hawley, Alex Turner and I Blame Coco {{!}} Pop reviews |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/nov/22/richard-hawley-alex-turner-coco |access-date=11 August 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=22 November 2009 }}</ref> Homme joined Arctic Monkeys for a live performance in [[Pioneertown, California]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Alex Turner: 'If I could play guitar like Josh Homme, I f**king would' |url=https://www.gigwise.com/news/84334/ |access-date=12 August 2018 |work=www.gigwise.com}}</ref>

Turner wrote Arctic Monkeys' fourth album, ''[[Suck It and See]]'', in New York<ref>{{cite news |last1=Norris |first1=John |date=31 May 2011 |title=Craft Service: Arctic Monkeys Branch Out – Interview Magazine |url=https://www.interviewmagazine.com/music/arctic-monkeys-suck-it-and-see |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=Interview Magazine}}</ref> and met up with his bandmates and James Ford for recording sessions in Los Angeles. Marc Hogan of ''Pitchfork'' enjoyed the album's "chiming indie pop balladry" and "muscular glam-rock".<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys: Suck It and See Album Review |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/15450-suck-it-and-see/ |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com}}</ref> Petridis of ''The Guardian'' remarked that Turner's new lyrical style of "dense, [[Bob Dylan|Dylanesque]] wordplay is tough to get right. More often than not, he pulls it off. There are beautifully turned phrases and piercing observation."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |date=2 June 2011 |title=Arctic Monkeys: Suck It and See – review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2011/jun/02/arctic-monkeys-suck-it-and-see-review |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> Richard Hawley co-wrote and provided vocals for the B-side, "[[Black Treacle|You and I]]", and performed the song with the band at the Olympia in Paris.<ref>{{cite news |date=20 January 2012 |title=Check Out: Arctic Monkeys feat. Richard Hawley "You & I" |url=https://consequence.net/2012/01/check-out-arctic-monkeys-featuring-richard-hawley-you-i/ |access-date=11 August 2018 |work=Consequence of Sound}}</ref> Turner joined [[Elvis Costello]] on stage in New York to sing "[[Lipstick Vogue]]".

==== International breakthrough ====
By 2012, Arctic Monkeys were based in [[Los Angeles]], with Turner and Helders sharing a house.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys go California |url=http://www.sfexaminer.com/arctic-monkeys-go-california/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180811032615/http://www.sfexaminer.com/arctic-monkeys-go-california/ |archive-date=11 August 2018 |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=The San Francisco Examiner}}</ref> Arctic Monkeys toured the US as the support act for [[the Black Keys]] in early 2012. While they had previously opened for [[Oasis (band)|Oasis]] and Queens of the Stone Age at one-off shows, it was the band's first time to tour as a supporting act.<ref>{{cite news |title=Q&a Arctic Monkeys' Matt Helders – On touring the US with The Black Keys, life as a support band, getting love from Metallica & new album plans |url=https://www.qthemusic.com/articles/interviews/qa-arctic-monkeys-matt-helders-touring-us-black-keys-life-support-band-getting-love-metallica-new-album-plans |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=Q Magazine}}</ref> They released "[[R U Mine?]]" as a standalone single in preparation for the tour. Later that year, Arctic Monkeys performed "I Bet You Look Good on the Dancefloor" and a cover of "[[Come Together]]" by [[the Beatles]] at the [[2012 Summer Olympics opening ceremony|2012 London Summer Olympics opening ceremony]]. In early 2013, Turner provided backing vocals for the Queens of the Stone Age song "[[...Like Clockwork|If I Had a Tail]]"<ref>{{cite web |title=Kalopsia by Queens of the Stone Age |url=http://www.songfacts.com/detail.php?id=29675 |access-date=8 April 2014 |website=Songfacts.com}}</ref> and played bass guitar on "[[Don't Forget Who You Are (song)|Get Right]]", a Miles Kane B-side.<ref>{{cite web |date=3 June 2013 |title=Miles Kane – Don't Forget Who You Are (Vinyl) |url=http://www.discogs.com/Miles-Kane-Dont-Forget-Who-You-Are/release/4627366 |access-date=8 April 2014 |website=[[Discogs]]}}</ref> Arctic Monkeys headlined Glastonbury Festival for a second time in June.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Beaumont |first1=Mark |date=29 June 2013 |title=Arctic Monkeys at Glastonbury 2013 – review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2013/jun/29/arctic-monkeys-2013-glastonbury-review |access-date=26 July 2018 |website=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Savage |first1=Mark |date=29 June 2013 |title=Arctic Monkeys headline Glastonbury |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-23094281 |access-date=5 August 2018 |work=BBC News}}</ref>

''[[AM (Arctic Monkeys album)|AM]]'' was released in September 2013.<ref name=undertheradarmag>{{cite news |last1=Fink |first1=Matt |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner on "AM," Working with Josh Homme, and Adapting John Cooper Clarke |url=http://www.undertheradarmag.com/interviews/arctic_monkeys1/ |access-date=14 August 2018 |work=www.undertheradarmag.com |date=8 November 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=9 March 2012 |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner: 'I want to start writing follow-up to 'Suck It And See' |url=https://www.nme.com/news/arctic-monkeys/62517 |access-date=28 June 2013 |website=NME}}</ref><ref name="TheFly">{{cite magazine |last=Dunning |first=J.&nbsp;J. |date=September 2013 |title=Arctic Monkeys are all over the place |magazine=[[The Fly (magazine)|The Fly]]}}</ref> Ryan Dombal of ''Pitchfork'' said that the album, dealing with "desperate 3 a.m. thoughts", managed to modernise "[[T. Rex (band)|T. Rex]] bop, [[Bee Gees]] backup vocals, [[The Rolling Stones|Rolling Stones]] R&B, and [[Black Sabbath]] monster riffage".<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys: AM Album Review {{!}} Pitchfork |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/18386-arctic-monkeys-am/ |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com}}</ref> Phil Mongredien of ''The Guardian'' described it as "their most coherent, most satisfying album since their debut": "Turner proves he has not lost his knack for an insightful lyric."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mongredien |first1=Phil |date=7 September 2013 |title=Arctic Monkeys: AM – review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2013/sep/08/arctic-monkeys-am-review |access-date=24 July 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> Arctic Monkeys promoted the album heavily in the US, in contrast to previous album campaigns where, according to Helders, they had refused to do radio promotion: "We couldn't even have told you why at the time. Just stubborn teenage thinking."<ref name="hiatt" /> Arctic Monkeys spent 18 months touring ''AM''; they were joined onstage by [[Josh Homme]] in both Los Angeles and Austin.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Cubarrubia |first1=R.&nbsp;J. |date=4 October 2013 |title=Josh Homme Joins Arctic Monkeys Onstage |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/josh-homme-joins-arctic-monkeys-onstage-84439/ |access-date=11 August 2018 |magazine=Rolling Stone |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180812022646/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/josh-homme-joins-arctic-monkeys-onstage-84439/ |archive-date=12 August 2018 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Webb |first1=Jacob |date=13 October 2013 |title=Live Review: Austin City Limits 2013, Friday |url=https://blog.kexp.org/2013/10/13/live-review-austin-city-limits-2013-friday/ |access-date=12 August 2018 |work=The KEXP Blog}}</ref>[[File:RoyalAlbertHall2018.jpg|thumb|Turner performing at the [[Royal Albert Hall]] in 2018|194x194px]]
''[[Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino]]'', Arctic Monkeys' sixth album, was released in May 2018.<ref>{{cite web |last=Trendell |first=Andrew |date=5 April 2018 |title=Arctic Monkeys announce new album 'Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino' |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-announce-new-album-tranquility-base-hotel-casino-2283051 |access-date=5 April 2018 |website=NME}}</ref> After receiving a [[Steinway Vertegrand]] piano as a 30th birthday present from his manager, Turner wrote the space-themed album from the perspective of "a lounge-y character".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Newstead |first1=Al |date=11 May 2018 |title=Alex Turner unpacks Arctic Monkeys' subversive, sci-fi referencing new album |url=https://www.abc.net.au/triplej/news/musicnews/arctic-monkeys-tranquility-base-hotel-casino-feature-alex-turner/9747272 |access-date=13 July 2018 |work=triple j}}</ref><ref name="billboard.com">{{cite magazine |last1=Havens |first1=Lyndsey |title=Inside Arctic Monkeys' New Album: Alex Turner Discusses Why Swapping Guitar for Piano Led to the Band's Most Ambitious Album |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rock/inside-arctic-monkeys-tranquility-base-hotel-casino-alex-turner-interview-8357304/ |access-date=15 August 2018 |magazine=Billboard |date=20 April 2018}}</ref> He recorded demos at home, alone and later with Helders,<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys Drummer Matt Helders on Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino and Drumming with Restraint |url=https://www.kexp.org/read/2018/10/23/arctic-monkeys-drummer-matt-helders-tranquility-base-hotel-and-casino-and-drumming-restraint/ |access-date=15 January 2020 |work=www.kexp.org}}</ref> and shared them with Cook in early 2017. Cook was initially taken-aback by the change in direction but was "very, very excited by what he'd come up with."<ref name="Rolling Stone">{{cite magazine |last1=Weiner |first1=Jonah |date=3 May 2018 |title=Arctic Monkeys Start Over: Inside Their Weird New Sci-Fi Opus |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/arctic-monkeys-start-over-628863/ |access-date=13 July 2018 |magazine=Rolling Stone}}</ref> By mid–2017, the whole band was recording the project, produced by Turner and James Ford, in both Los Angeles and France.<ref name="billboard.com"/> They were joined by musicians from the bands [[Tame Impala]], [[Klaxons]], and Mini Mansions.<ref>{{cite news |title=NME Big Read: Arctic Monkeys 'from the Ritz to the Hubble' |url=https://www.nme.com/big-read-arctic-monkeys-science-fiction-allows-explore-real-world |access-date=14 July 2018 |work=NME}}</ref>

Upon release, Jonah Weiner of ''Rolling Stone'' characterised ''Tranquility Base'' as "a captivatingly bizarre album about the role of entertainment – the desire to escape into it, and the desire to create it – during periods of societal upheaval and crisis."<ref name="Rolling Stone" /> Alexis Petridis of ''The Guardian'' found it "quietly impressive" that the band chose to release the "thrilling, smug, clever and oddly cold album" rather than more crowd-pleasing fare.<ref name="Petridis Guardian">{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |date=10 May 2018 |title=Arctic Monkeys: Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino review – funny, fresh and a little smug {{!}} Alexis Petridis' album of the week |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2018/may/10/arctic-monkeys-tranquility-hotel-base-casino-review |access-date=13 July 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> Jazz Monroe of ''Pitchfork'' declared it "a delirious and artful satire directed at the foundations of modern society."<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys: Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino Album Review {{!}} Pitchfork |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/arctic-monkeys-tranquility-base-hotel-and-casino/ |access-date=14 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com}}</ref> The album became the eighth number one album of Turner's career in the UK.<ref>{{cite news |date=18 May 2018 |title=Arctic Monkeys set new chart record with massive sales as 'Tranquility Base Hotel + Casino' tops UK album chart – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-set-new-record-with-massive-sales-2319102 |access-date=15 July 2018 |work=NME}}</ref> The band [[Tranquility Base Hotel & Casino Tour|toured the album]] from May 2018 to April 2019.<ref>{{cite news |title=Here's everywhere you can see Arctic Monkeys live in 2018 |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-first-2018-tour-date-announced-theyre-teasing-2218077 |access-date=15 July 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=8 April 2019 |title=Arctic Monkeys have played their last show "until the next record" |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-played-last-show-next-record-2473932 |access-date=10 January 2020 |work=NME}}</ref>

After unsuccessfully attempting to write heavier guitar riffs for the next album, Turner's songwriting took a similar subdued tone on Arctic Monkeys' seventh studio album, ''[[The Car (album)|The Car]]'', which was released in October 2022.<ref>{{cite web |last=Monroe |first=Jazz |date=24 August 2022 |title=Arctic Monkeys Announce New Album The Car |url=https://pitchfork.com/news/arctic-monkeys-announce-new-album-the-car/ |access-date=24 August 2022 |work=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Skinner |first=Tom |date=24 August 2022 |title=Arctic Monkeys announce their seventh studio album, 'The Car' |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-announce-new-seventh-album-the-car-tracklist-artwork-preorder-3296756 |access-date=24 August 2022 |work=[[NME]]}}</ref> The album heavily featured a string section co-arranged by Turner and its lead single, "[[There'd Better Be a Mirrorball]]", was accompanied by a video co-directed by Turner.<ref name="CarNME">{{cite web |last1=Smith |first1=Thomas |date=18 October 2022 |title=Arctic Monkeys – 'The Car' review: a swashbuckling, strings-fuelled epic |url=https://www.nme.com/reviews/album/arctic-monkeys-the-car-review-3330197 |access-date=2 July 2023 |website=[[NME]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Renshaw |first=David |date=30 August 2022 |title=Arctic Monkeys share new song "There'd Better Be A Mirrorball" |url=https://www.thefader.com/2022/08/30/arctic-monkeys-thered-better-be-a-mirrorball |access-date=30 August 2022 |work=[[The Fader]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Robinson |first=Ellie |date=29 August 2022 |title=Listen to Arctic Monkeys' first single in four years, 'There'd Better be a Mirrorball' |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/listen-to-arctic-monkeys-first-single-in-four-years-thered-better-be-a-mirrorball-3300149 |access-date=30 August 2022 |work=[[NME]]}}</ref> In 2023, the band headlined [[Glastonbury Festival 2023|Glastonbury Festival]] for the third time, despite Turner having been diagnosed with acute [[laryngitis]] days before.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bullen |first=Jamie |date=23 June 2023 |title=Arctic Monkeys to headline Glastonbury after Dublin gig cancelled |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2023/06/23/glastonbury-arctic-monkeys-headline-alex-turner-laryngitis/ |access-date=29 June 2023 |work=The Telegraph}}</ref>

=== The Last Shadow Puppets ===
[[File:The Last Shadow Puppets (Sound Fix) 006 (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|Turner performing with [[the Last Shadow Puppets]] in 2008|197x197px]]
Turner announced plans in 2007 to form a side-project band, [[the Last Shadow Puppets]], with Ford and [[Miles Kane]], whom he had befriended during a tour in mid-2005.<ref name=exlaim/><ref>{{cite web |last=Fletcher |first=Alex |date=2 August 2007 |title=Arctic Monkey plans side project |url=https://www.digitalspy.com/music/a69219/arctic-monkey-plans-side-project/ |access-date=29 September 2014 |website=[[Digital Spy]]}}</ref> The Last Shadow Puppets' debut album, ''[[The Age of the Understatement]]'', was released in April 2008, shortly after Turner had moved from Sheffield to east London.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cobb |first1=Ben |date=7 June 2017 |title=Alex Turner |url=http://www.anothermanmag.com/library/9877/alex-turner |access-date=17 August 2018 |work=AnotherMan}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=22 February 2008 |title=Reverend and The Makers' Jon McClure gets it all off his chest |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/lifestyle/going-out/music/reverend-and-the-makers-jon-mcclure-gets-294607 |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=The Mirror}}</ref> Co-written by Turner and Kane, the album was recorded in the [[Loire Valley]], France<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hermann |first1=Andy |date=28 March 2016 |title=The Last Shadow Puppets Joke Around About Everything, Except Their Music |url=https://www.laweekly.com/music/the-last-shadow-puppets-joke-around-about-everything-except-their-music-6753834 |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=L.A. Weekly}}</ref> and featured string arrangements by [[Owen Pallett]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Thompson |first1=Ben |date=16 March 2008 |title=What do you get when you mix one Arctic Monkey and one young Rascal, asks Ben Thompson |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2008/mar/16/features.musicmonthly19 |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> Hogan of ''Pitchfork'' noted that, lyrically, Turner was "moving from his anthropologically detailed Arctics brushstrokes to bold, cinematic gestures."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hogan |first1=Marc |title=The Last Shadow Puppets: The Age of the Understatement Album Review {{!}} Pitchfork |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/11433-the-age-of-the-understatement/ |access-date=4 August 2018 |work=Pitchfork}}</ref> Petridis of ''The Guardian'' detected "the audible enthusiasm of an artist broadening his scope" and praised "a certain fearlessness on display".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |date=17 April 2008 |title=CD of the week: The Last Shadow Puppets, The Age of the Understatement |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2008/apr/18/popandrock.shopping |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> During a tour with the [[London Philharmonic Orchestra]], Turner said Kane's presence gave him "somewhere to hide" on stage.<ref>{{cite news |title=Monkey business |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/arts-culture/music/monkey-business-1.509641 |access-date=10 February 2020 |work=The National}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Green |first=Thomas&nbsp;H. |date=19 August 2008 |title=The Last Shadow Puppets review: satisfied relief |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/music/3558820/The-Last-Shadow-Puppets-review-satisfied-relief.html |access-date=22 September 2008 |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London}}</ref> The Last Shadow Puppets gave a surprise performance at Glastonbury Festival, with both Matt Helders and [[Jack White]] making guest appearances.<ref name="secretglastonbury">{{cite news |date=28 June 2008 |title=Last Shadow Puppets and Jack White play secret Glastonbury gig – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/the-last-shadow-puppets-62-1324065 |access-date=7 August 2018 |work=NME}}</ref> [[Alison Mosshart]] performed with the band at the [[Olympia (Paris)|Olympia]] in Paris, and provided vocals for a [[My Mistakes Were Made For You|B-side]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Thompson |first1=Paul |title=Last Shadow Puppets Enlist Kills' Mosshart for EP {{!}} Pitchfork |url=https://pitchfork.com/news/33830-last-shadow-puppets-enlist-kills-mosshart-for-ep/ |access-date=11 August 2018 |work=pitchfork.com}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=12 August 2008 |title=The Kills discuss Arctic Monkeys collaboration – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/the-kills-42-1323347 |access-date=11 August 2018 |work=NME}}</ref> Also in 2008, Turner formed a [[Cover band|covers band]] with [[Dev Hynes]] for a one-off show in London<ref>{{cite news |last1=Duff |first1=Oliver |date=1 February 2008 |title=How all hell broke loose when two indie icons celebrated their past |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/reviews/how-all-hell-broke-loose-when-two-indie-icons-celebrated-their-past-776553.html |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180803183542/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/reviews/how-all-hell-broke-loose-when-two-indie-icons-celebrated-their-past-776553.html |archive-date=3 August 2018 |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=The Independent}}</ref> and recorded a spoken word track "A Choice of Three" for Helders' compilation album ''[[Late Night Tales: Matt Helders|Late Night Tales]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Michaels |first1=Sean |date=5 September 2008 |title=Alex Turner to make spoken-word debut |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2008/sep/05/alex.turner.spoken.word.debut |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref>

The Last Shadow Puppets released their second album, ''[[Everything You've Come to Expect]]'', in April 2016. Turner, Kane and Ford were joined by [[Zach Dawes]] of [[Mini Mansions]], with whom Turner had collaborated on the songs "[[The Great Pretenders|Vertigo]]" and "I Love You All The Time" in 2015.<ref>{{cite web |last=Renshaw |first=David |date=10 March 2015 |title=Mini Mansions song with Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner released online |url=https://www.nme.com/news/mini-mansions/83384 |access-date=10 March 2015 |website=NME}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=20 December 2015 |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner appears on Eagles of Death Metal charity cover – listen {{!}} NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-8-1200709 |access-date=19 January 2020 |work=NME}}</ref> Owen Pallett again composed the string arrangements, this time working in the studio with the band rather than remotely.<ref>{{cite web |last=Grand-Pierre |first=Ken |date=15 February 2017 |title=The Last Shadow Puppets |url=https://www.qromag.com/interviews/the-last-shadow-puppets/ |access-date=19 January 2020}}</ref> According to Turner, the album featured "the most straight-up love letters" of his career, written for American model [[Taylor Bagley]] whom he dated from 2015 to 2018.<ref name="auto">MOJO June 2018, page 79, Andrew Cottirill</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Distefano |first1=Alex |date=28 June 2018 |title=R.I.P. Steve Soto of The Adolescents, Manic Hispanic |url=http://www.laweekly.com/music/rip-steve-soto-of-the-adolescents-manic-hispanic-9607384 |access-date=4 July 2018 |website=L.A. Weekly}}</ref> Laura Snapes of ''Pitchfork'' detected an air of "misanthropy" in the album. However, she acknowledged that Turner was "no less a gifted lyricist than ever" and described some songs as "totally gorgeous ... the structures fluid and surprising".<ref>{{cite news |title=The Last Shadow Puppets: Everything You've Come to Expect Album Review {{!}} Pitchfork |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/21596-everything-youve-come-to-expect/ |access-date=17 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com}}</ref> Alexis Petridis of ''The Guardian'' enjoyed Turner's "characteristically sparkling use of language" and "melodic skill". However, he felt the pair's "in-joking" during interviews and Kane's "leery" encounter with a female ''Spin'' journalist sullied the album.<ref name="Petridis 2016 ">{{cite news |last1=Petridis |first1=Alexis |date=24 March 2016 |title=The Last Shadow Puppets: Everything You've Come to Expect review – more a smirking in-joke than a band |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2016/mar/24/the-last-shadow-puppets-everything-youve-come-to-expect-review-more-a-smirking-in-joke-than-a-band |access-date=17 July 2018 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> From March until August 2016, they toured in Europe and North America.<ref>{{cite web |title=The Last Shadow Puppets – The Dream Synopsis EP |url=http://www.thelastshadowpuppets.com/#gigs |access-date=17 December 2016 |website=The Last Shadow Puppets – Everything You've Come To Expect}}</ref> Turner's father David played saxophone at a Berlin show.<ref>{{cite news |date=24 August 2016 |title=Alex Turner's Dad Played Sax At A Recent Last Shadow Puppets Gig And it Will Make You Weak At The Knees |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/alex-turners-dad-played-sax-at-a-recent-last-shadow-puppets-gig-and-it-will-make-you-weak-at-the-kne-3990 |access-date=15 January 2020 |work=NME}}</ref>

=== Solo work and collaborations ===
While living in [[Brooklyn|Brooklyn, New York]], where he had moved in the spring of 2009,<ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> Turner wrote an acoustic soundtrack for the coming-of-age feature film ''[[Submarine (2010 film)|Submarine]]'' (2010);<ref>{{cite news |title=Periscope up: Richard Ayoade and Alex Turner unite their talents in |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/films/features/periscope-up-richard-ayoade-and-alex-turner-unite-their-talents-in-hot-new-brit-flick-submarine-2243847.html |access-date=23 July 2018 |work=The Independent}}</ref> it was released as [[Submarine (EP)|an EP]] in March 2011.<ref>{{cite news |title=Alex Turner – Official Chart History |url=http://www.officialcharts.com/artist/6806/alex-turner/ |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=Official Charts Company |date=26 March 2011 }}</ref> Director Richard Ayoade initially approached Turner to sing cover versions<ref name=GQ>{{cite web |title=Alex Turner: GQ Music Issue 2011: The Survivors |url=https://www.gq.com/story/alex-turner-gq-music-issue |website=GQ |access-date=13 July 2018 |date=15 November 2011}}</ref> but, instead, he recorded six original songs in London, accompanied by James Ford and [[Bill Ryder-Jones]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.parttimewizards.co.uk/bill-ryder-jones-interview/ |title=Bill Ryder Jones – Interview |website=Part Time Wizards |access-date=26 August 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150914052638/http://www.parttimewizards.co.uk/bill-ryder-jones-interview/ |archive-date=14 September 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.yourmovemagazine.com/bill-ryder-jones-former-the-coral-guitarist-and-solo-artist/483 |title=Bill-Ryder Jones – former The Coral guitarist and solo artist |date=2 August 2014 |website=Your Move Magazine |access-date=26 August 2015 |archive-date=27 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160827171348/http://www.yourmovemagazine.com/bill-ryder-jones-former-the-coral-guitarist-and-solo-artist/483 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Two of the songs had already been written; Turner wrote the rest after watching [[dailies]] from the film set.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/films/features/periscope-up-richard-ayoade-and-alex-turner-unite-their-talents-in-hot-new-brit-flick-submarine-2243847.html |title=Periscope up: Richard Ayoade and Alex Turner unite their talents in hot new Brit flick Submarine |newspaper=[[The Independent]] |date=20 March 2011 |access-date=14 April 2020}}</ref> The songs existed within the world of the film as a mixtape made by the main character's father.<ref>{{cite news|first=John |last=DeFore |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/style/submarine-is-director-richard-ayoades-coming-of-age/2011/06/07/AGvFYkNH_story.html |title='Submarine' is director Richard Ayoade's coming of age |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=9 June 2011 |access-date=14 April 2020}}</ref> Paul Thompson of ''Pitchfork'' felt "Turner's keen wit and eye for detail" had created a "tender portrayal" of adolescent uncertainty.<ref>{{cite news |title=Alex Turner: Submarine OST Album Review |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/15224-submarine-ost/ |access-date=13 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com }}</ref>  Ben Walsh of ''The Independent'' said the "exquisite" soundtrack was "reminiscent" of [[Cat Stevens]]'s work on ''[[Harold and Maude]]''.<ref>{{cite news |title=What happened to movie music? |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/films/features/what-happened-to-movie-music-2331831.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111001025635/http://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/films/features/what-happened-to-movie-music-2331831.html |archive-date=1 October 2011 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |access-date=13 February 2020 |work=The Independent |date=5 August 2011 }}</ref> In 2014, the ''Submarine'' soundtrack appeared on ''The Times'''s list of 100 Soundtracks to Love.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thetimes.com/sunday-times-rich-list/profile/article/100-soundtracks-to-love-nh989zmz3fk |title=100 soundtracks to love &#124; The Sunday Times |website=The Times |date=9 November 2014 |access-date=14 April 2020}}</ref> Turner also co-wrote six songs for Miles Kane's debut solo album ''[[Colour of the Trap]]'' (2011) and co-wrote Kane's standalone single "[[First of My Kind]]" (2012).<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ascap.com/repertory#ace/search/title/First%20Of%20My%20Kind/performer/Miles%20Kane |title=First Of My Kind / Miles Kane |website=ASCAP |access-date=23 May 2018}}</ref>

[[Columbia Records]] approached Turner about working with [[Alexandra Savior]] in 2014, and he co-wrote her debut album, ''[[Belladonna of Sadness (album)|Belladonna of Sadness]]'', in between Arctic Monkeys' touring commitments. Turner and James Ford co-produced the album in 2015, with Turner also playing bass, guitars, keyboards, and synthesisers.<ref name="Coup De Main Magazine">{{cite news |last1=Graves |first1=Shahlin |title=Interview: Alexandra Savior on her upcoming debut album. |url=https://www.coupdemainmagazine.com/alexandra-savior/11541 |access-date=16 July 2018 |work=Coup De Main Magazine |date=10 October 2016 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Alexandra Savior On Songwriting, Working With Alex Turner & Visiting Australia – Music Feeds |url=https://musicfeeds.com.au/features/alexandra-savior-on-songwriting-working-with-alex-turner-visting-australia/#1GItEkDXrbuvD4HP.99 |access-date=16 July 2018 |work=Music Feeds |date=13 April 2017}}</ref> An additional song "Risk" was recorded with [[T Bone Burnett]] for an episode of the crime drama ''[[True Detective]]''.<ref>{{cite news |title=Meet Alexandra Savior: The LA Songwriter Who's Made An Entire Album With Alex Turner |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-radar/meet-alex-turners-new-muse-alexandra-savior-8134 |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=NME |date=16 May 2016}}</ref> While Turner and Savior performed together in Los Angeles in 2016,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-radar/meet-alex-turners-new-muse-alexandra-savior-8134|title=Check Out Alexandra Savior, Alex Turner's New Songwriting Partner|date=16 May 2016 |access-date=16 July 2018|work=NME}}</ref> the album was not released until April 2017. In reviewing it, Hilary Hughes of ''Pitchfork'' remarked: "Turner's musical ticks are so distinct that they're instantly recognizable when someone else tries to dress them up as their own."<ref>{{cite news |title=Alexandra Savior: Belladonna of Sadness Album Review |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/23176-belladonna-of-sadness/ |access-date=16 July 2018 |work=pitchfork.com }}</ref> Savior later said the press attention surrounding Turner's involvement was overwhelming: "I'm so grateful for him, but I'm also like, 'Alright, alright!'"<ref name="Coup De Main Magazine"/>

In 2022, Turner composed the music for the audiobook version of [[Richard Ayoade]]'s ''The Book That No One Wanted to Read'' with the instrumental interludes being performed himself and James Ford.<ref>{{cite web |date=10 November 2022 |title=ACE REPERTORY |url=https://www.ascap.com/repertory#/ace/search/title/NAGRA%20(THE%20BOOK%20THAT%20NO%20ONE%20WANTED%20TO%20READ?at=false&searchFilter=SVW&page=1 |access-date=30 January 2023 |work=ACE Repertory – ASCAP}}</ref> He also composed the music for the audiobook version of Ayoade's subsequent book, ''The Unfinished Harauld Hughes'', released in 2024.<ref>{{cite web |last=Spanoudi|first=Melina|date=15 July 2024 |title=Faber nabs The Unfinished Harauld Hughes by Richard Ayoade |url=https://www.thebookseller.com/rights/faber-nabs-the-unfinished-harauld-hughes-by-richard-ayoade |access-date=15 July 2024 |work=[[The Bookseller]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240715181708/https://www.thebookseller.com/rights/faber-nabs-the-unfinished-harauld-hughes-by-richard-ayoade|archive-date=15 July 2024}}</ref>

==Artistry==
===Influences===
[[File:Alex Turner (musician) 2011.jpg|thumb|Turner performing in 2011|202x202px]]
Turner was "into hip-hop in a big way" as a teenager.<ref name="pitchfork.com"/> When he first started writing lyrics, [[Roots Manuva]]'s ''[[Run Come Save Me]]'' was his main influence.<ref name=laurenmurphy/><ref name="spin.com"/> He also listened to [[Rawkus Records]] and [[Lyricist Lounge]] compilations,<ref>{{cite news |title='We'll Be Dropped Within A Year' – Read Arctic Monkeys' First NME Cover Feature {{!}} NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/well-be-dropped-within-a-year-read-arctic-monkeys-first-nme-cover-feature-761969 |access-date=12 January 2020 |work=NME |date=5 April 2011}}</ref> and artists such as [[Dr. Dre]],<ref name="spin.com">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.spin.com/2013/09/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-interview-am-album/ |title=The SPIN Interview: Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner |first=David |last=Bevan |date=10 September 2013 |magazine=[[Spin (magazine)|Spin]] |access-date=26 August 2015}}</ref> [[Snoop Dogg]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Believe the hype: Arctic Monkeys rock |url=https://www.today.com/popculture/believe-hype-arctic-monkeys-rock-wbna11522655 |access-date=13 August 2018 |work=TODAY.com |date=23 February 2006}}</ref> [[Outkast]], [[Eminem]]<ref name="pitchfork.com"/> and [[the Streets]].<ref name="spin.com"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Taylor |first1=Chris |title=Arctic Monkeys Formed By Hip-Hop Love |url=https://www.gigwise.com/news/15388/ |access-date=14 August 2018 |work=www.gigwise.com |date=2 April 2006}}</ref> He has repeatedly cited [[Method Man]] as one of his favourite lyricists,<ref name="pitchfork.com"/><ref name="spin.com"/><ref name="ryzik">{{cite news |last1=Ryzik |first1=Melena |title=Alex Turner on Arctic Monkeys' 'Suck It and See' |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/05/15/arts/music/alex-turner-on-arctic-monkeys-suck-it-and-see.html |access-date=13 August 2018 |work=The New York Times |date=13 May 2011 }}</ref> and has referenced the [[Wu-Tang Clan]] in his own lyrics.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hyden |first1=Steven |title=Arctic Monkeys Grow Up and Get Weird On Their Inspired New Album |url=https://uproxx.com/music/arctic-monkeys-tranquility-base-hotel-and-casino-review/2/ |access-date=13 August 2018 |work=UPROXX |date=10 May 2018}}</ref>

For Turner, [[the Strokes]] were "that one band that comes along when you are 14 or 15 years old that manages to hit you in just the right way and changes your whole perception of things."<ref name="stereogum.com"/> He changed his style of dress and began to take an interest in guitar music.<ref name="strokes">{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Thomas |title=Alex Turner loves The Strokes even more than you do – here's how a certain bromance unfolded – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/alex-turner-loves-the-strokes-2359065#zeTzHL2WciiKZGxu.99 |access-date=12 August 2018 |work=NME |date=25 July 2018}}</ref> He has since referenced the band in his lyrics.<ref name="strokes"/> [[The Vines (band)|The Vines]] were the first band Turner ever saw live and [[Craig Nicholls]] provided inspiration for his early stage persona.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/mar/05/popandrock |title=Stop making sense |first=Craig |last=McLean |newspaper=The Observer |date=5 March 2006|access-date=22 August 2015}}</ref> Other early guitar influences included [[the Libertines]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gordon |first1=Scott |title=Random Rules: Alex Turner of Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.avclub.com/random-rules-alex-turner-of-arctic-monkeys-1798211451 |access-date=12 August 2018 |work=AV Club |date=22 May 2007}}</ref> [[the Coral]],<ref>{{cite news |title=The Coral speak about hero worship from Arctic Monkeys – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/the-coral-36-1327746 |access-date=13 August 2018 |work=NME |date=30 July 2008}}</ref> [[The Hives]]<ref name="Welcome to the jungle"/> and [[The White Stripes]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Trendell |first1=Andrew |title=Here's Arctic Monkeys covering The White Stripes in Detroit last night – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/heres-arctic-monkeys-covering-white-stripes-detroit-last-night-2362491 |access-date=13 August 2018 |work=NME |date=2 August 2018}}</ref> In his late teens, Turner began "delving" into older music and discovered lyricists including [[Elvis Costello]],<ref name="americansongwriter.com"/> [[Ray Davies]] of [[The Kinks]],<ref name="americansongwriter.com"/><ref name="pitchfork.com"/>  [[Jarvis Cocker]] of [[Pulp (band)|Pulp]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys diss 'annoying' Kaiser Chiefs – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-587-1366976 |access-date=15 August 2018 |work=NME |date=27 October 2005}}</ref> [[Paul Weller]] of [[The Jam]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Harris |first1=John |title=John Harris asks why pop lyrics are in such decline |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2005/jun/24/1 |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=24 June 2005 }}</ref> and [[Morrissey]] of [[The Smiths]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Alex Turner – The Record(s) That Changed My Life – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/alex-turner-the-records-that-changed-my-life-770704 |access-date=14 August 2018 |work=NME |date=25 April 2012}}</ref><ref name=GQ/> Turner has since performed with [[Jack White]] of The White Stripes,<ref name="secretglastonbury"/> Costello and [[Johnny Marr]] of The Smiths.<ref>{{cite news |title='He can't destroy history': Johnny Marr on veganism, Man City and Morrissey's legacy |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/live/2018/jun/11/johnny-marr-webchat-post-your-questions-now |access-date=14 August 2018 |work=The Guardian |date=14 June 2018}}</ref>

[[John Cooper Clarke]], whose poetry Turner first encountered at school, was a "massive" source of early inspiration.<ref name=undertheradarmag/><ref name="schoolteachers"/> Turner was working as a barman at The Boardwalk in Sheffield in late 2004 when Clarke appeared on stage as the opening act for [[The Fall (band)|the Fall]].<ref name="Pitchfork-09-13"/> The performance made a big impression on the eighteen-year-old: "He's talking 100 miles an hour, and he's really funny ... It just blew my mind."<ref name="spin.com"/> He was inspired by Clarke's use of a regional accent and the early Arctic Monkeys song "From the Ritz to the Rubble" was his homage to Clarke's style ("my best shot at it, at least").<ref name=EvidentlyJCC>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b01jcdbc |title=Evidently... John Cooper Clarke |date=15 December 2013 |website=[[BBC Four]] |access-date=28 March 2016}}</ref> Later in his career, Turner requested to interview Clarke for ''Mojo Magazine'', published two Clarke poems as part of a single's artwork<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys collaborate with punk legend – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-558-1344693 |access-date=3 August 2018 |work=NME |date=6 July 2007}}</ref> and used another ("I Wanna Be Yours") as the lyrical basis for a song.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hall |first1=Duncan |title=Punk poet John Cooper Clarke talks art, Arctic Monkeys and the possibility of an autobiography |url=https://www.theargus.co.uk/news/11156146.Punk_poet_John_Cooper_Clarke_talks_art__Arctic_Monkeys_and_the_possibility_of_an_autobiography/ |access-date=27 July 2018 |work=The Argus |date=18 April 2014 }}</ref> In 2018, Arctic Monkeys invited Clarke to perform his reading of "I Wanna Be Yours" at one of their Sheffield arena shows.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Krol |first1=Charlotte |title=Arctic Monkeys play 'Mardy Bum' for first time in four years at Sheffield homecoming show |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-play-mardy-bum-first-time-in-four-years-at-sheffield-homecoming-show-2382497 |access-date=4 January 2019 |work=NME |date=22 September 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Blackledge |first1=Richard |title=John Cooper Clarke interview: 'I hated school but teachers like me – I get their pupils interested in poetry' |url=https://www.thestar.co.uk/whats-on/out-and-about/john-cooper-clarke-interview-i-hated-school-but-teachers-like-me-i-get-their-pupils-interested-in-poetry-1-9425835 |access-date=4 January 2019 |work=www.thestar.co.uk }}</ref>

[[Nick Lowe]],<ref name="argentina">{{cite magazine |last1=Orellana |first1=Patricio |title=Alex Turner: "Creo que las cosas eran más cool en el pasado" – Rolling Stone Argentina |url=https://www.rollingstone.com.ar/1459775-alex-turner-creo-que-las-cosas-eran-mas-cool-en-el-pasado |access-date=15 August 2018 |magazine=Rolling Stone |date=26 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180815200956/http://www.rollingstone.com.ar/1459775-alex-turner-creo-que-las-cosas-eran-mas-cool-en-el-pasado |archive-date=15 August 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Jake Thackray]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Drinks With: Arctic Monkeys |url=https://americansongwriter.com/drinks-with-arctic-monkeys/skip-matheny/ |access-date=19 January 2020 |work=American Songwriter |date=12 March 2010 |archive-date=15 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191215191016/https://americansongwriter.com/drinks-with-arctic-monkeys/skip-matheny/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Nick Cave]],<ref name="argentina"/> [[John Cale]],<ref name="spin.com"/> [[Leonard Cohen]]<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/alex-turner-leonard-cohen-songwriting-2314586|title=Alex Turner says hearing Leonard Cohen talk about songwriting 'spurred him on' to write 'Tranquility Base Hotel + Casino'|work=NME|first=Rhian|last=Daly|date=14 May 2018|access-date=19 January 2020}}</ref> and [[Bob Dylan]]<ref>{{cite news |title=Dylan & Me |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/culture/music/2.681/dylan-me-1.580418 |access-date=2 February 2020 |newspaper=The Irish Times }}</ref> are among the lyricists Turner admires. He has spoken of his respect for country music songwriters like [[Roger Miller]], [[Willie Nelson]], [[Townes Van Zandt]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sweeney |first1=Eamon |title=Monkey business – Independent.ie |url=https://www.independent.ie/entertainment/monkey-business-26736842.html |access-date=15 August 2018 |work=The Irish Independent |date=27 May 2011 }}</ref> and [[Hank Williams]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys – Alex Turner's Guide To 'Suck It And See' {{!}} NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/arctic-monkeys-alex-turners-guide-to-suck-it-and-see-762359 |access-date=21 January 2020 |work=NME |date=26 April 2011}}</ref> He has been drawn to artists who reinvented themselves throughout their careers: "[[The Beatles]], [[David Bowie]]… the big ones."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uncut.co.uk/features/alex-turner-making-an-arctic-monkeys-album-is-not-an-easy-alchemy-72724/2/|title=Alex Turner: "Making an Arctic Monkeys album is not an easy alchemy"|date=11 May 2018|work=Uncut|first=John|last=Robinson|access-date=12 January 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Liam Gallagher, Alex Turner, Paul Weller And More On The True Genius Of John Lennon – NME |url=https://www.nme.com/blogs/nme-blogs/liam-gallagher-alex-turner-paul-weller-and-more-on-the-true-genius-of-john-lennon-770660 |access-date=17 August 2018 |work=NME |date=8 October 2015}}</ref> When forming The Last Shadow Puppets in 2007, Turner was inspired by the music of Bowie, [[Scott Walker (singer)|Scott Walker]], [[Serge Gainsbourg]], [[David Axelrod (musician)|David Axelrod]] and [[Ennio Morricone]].<ref>{{cite news |title=The Last Shadow PUPPETS |url=https://www.interviewmagazine.com/music/the-last-shadow-puppets |access-date=25 January 2020 |work=Interview Magazine |date=26 March 2016}}</ref> Songs that Turner has discussed repeatedly in interviews are [[Michael Chapman (singer)|Michael Chapman]]'s "You Say",<ref>{{cite news |title=Michael Chapman Album Reviews |url=http://www.michaelchapman.co.uk/cd2.htm |access-date=22 January 2020 |work=www.michaelchapman.co.uk}}</ref><ref name=laurenmurphy/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Seban |first1=Johanna |title=Alex Turner par Etienne Daho |url=https://www.lesinrocks.com/2013/09/05/musique/musique/alex-turner-etienne-daho/ |work=Les Inrocks |language=fr-FR|access-date=22 January 2020}}</ref> [[Leon Russell]]'s "[[A Song for You]]"<ref name="Brown vulture">{{cite news |last1=Brown |first1=Lane |title=How to Write a Great Rock Lyric |url=https://www.vulture.com/2018/08/how-to-write-a-great-rock-lyric.html |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=Vulture |date=10 August 2018 }}</ref><ref name=laurenmurphy/><ref>{{cite news |title=PLAYLIST: Alex Turner Lists More Influences On New Album |url=https://www.radiox.co.uk/artists/arctic-monkeys/alex-turner-lists-influences-new-album-playlist/ |access-date=10 August 2018 |work=Radio X |archive-date=24 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230124211137/https://www.radiox.co.uk/artists/arctic-monkeys/alex-turner-lists-influences-new-album-playlist/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> and [[Dion DiMucci|Dion]]'s "[[Born to Be with You (album)|Only You Know]]", describing the latter as "one of my favourite tunes of all time".<ref name="stokes"/><ref name="billboard.com"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Stolworthy |first1=Jacob |title=Alex Turner lists the songs that inspired the new Arctic Monkeys album |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/news/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-new-album-songs-inspiration-tranquility-base-hotel-casino-a8323256.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180426154057/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/news/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-new-album-songs-inspiration-tranquility-base-hotel-casino-a8323256.html |archive-date=26 April 2018 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |access-date=15 August 2018 |work=The Independent |date=26 April 2018}}</ref> He has described himself as a [[Beyoncé Knowles|Beyoncé]] fan.<ref>{{cite web|title= Alex Turner Admits He 'Loves' Beyonce Knowles|url= https://www.gigwise.com/news/32566/|website=GigWise|date=5 June 2007|access-date=13 March 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Old Romantic|website=[[Vogue Australia]]|first=Ella|last=Kemp|date=29 October 2022|pages=82–83|issue=November 2022}}</ref>

Turner's work is also influenced by movies. ''Tranquility Base Hotel and Casino'' was inspired by films such as [[2001: A Space Odyssey (film)|''2001: A Space Odyssey'']] (1968), ''[[World on a Wire]]'' (1973), ''[[Le Cercle Rouge]]'' (1970), ''[[Spirits of the Dead]]'' (1968), [[Inherent Vice (film)|''Inherent Vice'']] (2014) and ''[[The Last Waltz]]'' (1978).<ref name=":0">{{cite web |last=Azadeh |first=Darius |title=10 Films That Majorly Inspired Arctic Monkeys' Tranquility Base Hotel and Casino |url=https://www.tasteofcinema.com/2021/10-films-that-majorly-inspired-arctic-monkeys-tranquility-base-hotel-and-casino/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=Taste of Cinema – Movie Reviews and Classic Movie Lists |date=22 May 2021 }}</ref> In the song "[[Hello You]]", there are references to [[Postwar Britain (1945–1979)|post-war British]] movie ''[[Tread Softly Stranger]]'' (1958) and [[Stanley Kubrick]]'s unmade script for ''[[Stanley Kubrick's unrealized projects#Napoleon|Napoleon]]''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Arctic Monkeys – The Car track by track {{!}} X-Posure {{!}} Radio X | website=YouTube | date=20 October 2022 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8qX-vBSpLe0 |access-date=17 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Sharf |first=Zack |date=26 March 2018 |title=Stanley Kubrick's 'Napoleon': Behind the Scenes of the Greatest Epic Never Made (Exclusive) |url=https://www.indiewire.com/gallery/stanley-kubrick-napoleon-behind-the-scenes-photos/ |access-date=19 November 2022 |website=IndieWire }}</ref> He has also said he's interested in film production and editing and has read about those topics, citing [[In the Blink of an Eye (Murch book)|''In the Blink of an Eye'']] (2005) by [[Walter Murch]] as a book "that feels connected to the process and also the feel or lyrics on [''[[The Car (album)|The Car]]'']."<ref name="Lobb">{{cite web |last=Lobb |first=Adrian |date=29 September 2022 |title=Exclusive: 7 things we learned from Alex Turner about new Arctic Monkeys album The Car |url=https://www.bigissue.com/culture/music/exclusive-7-things-alex-turner-revealed-to-us-about-new-arctic-monkeys-album-the-car/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=The Big Issue }}</ref> Turner has also cited [[Raymond Chandler]]'s ''[[Philip Marlowe]]'' series<ref name="musikexpress.de">{{cite web |date=12 October 2022 |title=Der Musikexpress 11/2022 mit Björk, Arctic Monkeys & Die Nerven – die Themen im Überblick |url=https://www.musikexpress.de/?p=2199829 |access-date=5 December 2022 |website=Musikexpress |language=de-DE}}</ref> and works by authors [[David Foster Wallace]] and [[George Saunders]] as inspirations for ''The Car.''<ref name="ReferenceA">{{cite web |title=Arctic Monkeys over The Car en hun geheime Spotify-playlist {{!}} 3FM Exclusive {{!}} NPO 3FM | website=YouTube | date=11 October 2022 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OgeIf6AeY-c |access-date=5 December 2022}}</ref><ref name="deutschlandfunkkultur.de">{{cite web |last=deutschlandfunkkultur.de |title=Neues Album von Arctic Monkeys – Zeugnis der Entfremdung vom Showbusiness |url=https://www.deutschlandfunkkultur.de/arctic-monkeys-album-the-car-100.html |access-date=5 December 2022 |website=Deutschlandfunk Kultur |date=21 October 2022 |language=de}}</ref>

===Songwriting===
Kate Mossman of the ''New Statesman'' described Turner as "one of the great lyricists of the 21st century", writing that his songs are "full of lovingly extended metaphors" and "mordant Morrissey-style observations".<ref name=mossman>{{cite news |last1=Mossman |first1=Kate |title=Sometimes I wonder how Alex Turner can make being young sound so boring – maybe that's the point |url=https://www.newstatesman.com/music-and-performance/2013/09/sometimes-i-wonder-how-alex-turner-can-make-being-young-sound-so-borin |access-date=13 January 2020 |work=www.newstatesman.com }}</ref> Mike Laws of the ''Village Voice'' characterised him as "a writer without peer in virtually all of rock" and identified "rapid-fire prosody and facility with internal rhyme" as Turner's trademarks.<ref name=laws>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys Jettison Their Guitars and Shoot the Moon {{!}} The Village Voice |url=https://www.villagevoice.com/2018/05/11/arctic-monkeys-jettison-their-guitars-and-shoot-the-moon/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180511213334/https://www.villagevoice.com/2018/05/11/arctic-monkeys-jettison-their-guitars-and-shoot-the-moon/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=11 May 2018 |access-date=16 January 2020 |work=www.villagevoice.com}}</ref> [[Simon Armitage]], writing in ''The Guardian'',  said: "Of all those writing lyrics today, Turner is among the most poetic. His use of internal rhyme exists to be admired and envied ...  Turner is a storyteller and scene-setter."<ref name=armitage>{{cite news |last1=Armitage |first1=Simon |title=Simon Armitage on the Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2008/jun/27/arcticmonkeys.popandrock |work=The Guardian |date=26 June 2008|access-date=13 January 2020}}</ref> "Like all the estimable British lyricists, be it [[Noël Coward]] or Morrissey, Turner has always been willing to risk a delicious irony or witty turn of phrase, even in a sad song."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Armitage |first1=Simon |title=Simon Armitage meets Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/jul/12/arctic-monkeys-interviewed-simon-armitage |access-date=14 January 2020 |work=The Observer |date=11 July 2009}}</ref> Kitty Empire of ''The Observer'' considers him "probably the finest lyricist of his generation."<ref name="Empire guardian"/> Turner's frequent collaborator [[Owen Pallett]] said of him: "There are many contemporary rock songwriters who I admire, but none more than Alex, [...] there seems to be no limit to his lyrical inventiveness… It would be impossible to overpraise him."<ref>{{cite web |title='Submarine' at 10: an oral history of the Alex Turner-soundtracked indie |url=https://www.nme.com/features/submarine-at-10-anniversary-alex-turner-2898588 |website=NME |access-date=12 March 2023 |date=12 March 2021}}</ref>

Turner's early songs chronicled teenage nightlife in England,<ref name=mccormick>{{cite news |last1=McCormick |first1=Neil |title='That's me, they're singing my life' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/music/rockandjazzmusic/3650003/Thats-me-theyre-singing-my-life.html |access-date=18 January 2020 |date=9 February 2006}}</ref> and, according to Armitage, were "of the kitchen-sink, social-realism variety."<ref name=armitage/> [[Sasha Frere-Jones]] of the ''New Yorker'' described him as "a prodigy at both character sketches and song form."<ref name="New Yorker">{{cite magazine |last1=Frere-Jones |first1=Sasha |title=Blighted |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2006/06/05/blighted |access-date=2 February 2020 |magazine=The New Yorker }}</ref> Turner drew comparisons to [[Alan Bennett]]<ref>{{cite news |title=Poets of pop: a reply |url=https://www.newstatesman.com/music/2010/08/today-lyricists-pop-taylor |access-date=14 January 2020 |work=www.newstatesman.com }}</ref> and [[Victoria Wood]].<ref name=petridis2/> As Turner has aged, Laws of the ''Village Voice'' noted: "His lyrics have shied away from making themselves amenable to easy reading. [They have become] more oblique and abstruse — more apt, too, to adopt the perspective of somebody else entirely, and so more editorially unreliable."<ref name=laws/> Turner himself acknowledges that, after writing "so directly" on his early songs, he went through a period of "wanting to reject that and, you know… just be the walrus for a bit."<ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> He first "attempted to write lyrics that weren't so observational" with his side-project The Last Shadow Puppets, later remarking that he had sometimes veered too far "into abstraction",<ref>{{cite news |title=Interview: The Last Shadow Puppets |url=https://weallwantsomeone.org/2016/04/06/interview-the-last-shadow-puppets/ |access-date=26 January 2020 |work=We All Want Someone To Shout For |date=6 April 2016}}</ref> but that approach "definitely affected everything I wrote since".<ref name="GQ16"/> He considers the lyrics on 2018's ''Tranquility Base Hotel and Casino'' to be the most direct since his earliest songs: "I think that was something I was trying to get away from, and perhaps I've returned to it now."<ref>{{cite news |title=NME Big Read: Arctic Monkeys 'from the Ritz to the Hubble' |url=https://www.nme.com/big-reads/big-read-arctic-monkeys-science-fiction-allows-explore-real-world-2311629 |access-date=20 January 2020 |work=NME |date=10 May 2018}}</ref>

Turner has said his songs are preoccupied with romance,<ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> loneliness, and longing.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.esquire.com/uk/culture/news/a6017/arctic-monkeys-exclusive-photos-interview/ |title=How The Arctic Monkeys Saved Rock 'N' Roll |website=Esquire.com |date=1 January 1970 |access-date=14 April 2020 |archive-date=13 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220813102401/https://www.esquire.com/uk/culture/news/a6017/arctic-monkeys-exclusive-photos-interview/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Jazz Monroe of ''Drowned in Sound'' remarked: "Turner seems part of an elite club of songwriters whose best love songs are the requited ones."<ref>{{cite news |title=Album Review: Arctic Monkeys – AM |url=http://drownedinsound.com/releases/17825/reviews/4146818 |access-date=17 January 2020 |work=DrownedInSound |archive-date=28 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191228012311/http://drownedinsound.com/releases/17825/reviews/4146818 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Similarly, in reviewing 2013's ''AM'', Mossman found the depiction of romantic partners "two-dimensional. In "Fluorescent Adolescent", or the memorably titled "Mardy Bum", he somehow managed to tell a girl's side of the story even in the act of mocking her."<ref name=mossman/> Neil McCormick of ''The Telegraph'' noted "a particularly North of England, working-class quality of sarcastic misanthropy" in Turner's lyrics<ref name=mccormick/> while Frere-Jones of the ''New Yorker'' said Turner "manages to summon the intractable bleakness of someone three times his age".<ref name="New Yorker" /> Later songs have alluded to "questions of consumerism, hyper-reality, [and] accelerating technology".<ref name="www.msn.com">{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys review, O2 Arena, London: Indulgence is Alex Turner's secret weapon |url=https://www.msn.com/en-gb/entertainment/music/arctic-monkeys-review-o2-arena-london-indulgence-is-alex-turners-secret-weapon/ar-BBN7k79?li=BBoPJKU&srcref=rss&index=1&correct=1&answer=2 |access-date=26 January 2020 |work=www.msn.com}}</ref> Monroe of ''The Independent'' highlights "anti-industry sloganeering", "lyrical abstraction", "postmodern scepticism", and a "rejection of the entire rock construct" as the "through-line in Turner's work".<ref name="www.msn.com"/>

Turner himself says that his songwriting is "absolutely not" poetry: "Poetry and the written word are harder, you've no melodies to hide behind."<ref name="Monkey business"/><ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> After initial reluctance,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Armitage |first1=Simon |title=Simon Armitage meets Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/jul/12/arctic-monkeys-interviewed-simon-armitage |access-date=16 January 2020 |work=The Observer |date=11 July 2009}}</ref> he began publishing his lyrics with Arctic Monkeys' fourth album.<ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> In an in-depth conversation about songcraft with ''[[New York (magazine)|New York]]'''s Lane Brown, he said: "I don't really get the 'I wrote the whole song on the back of a cigarette packet in 20 minutes' sort of thing ... I would keep adding or changing words forever if somebody didn't stop me."<ref name="Brown vulture"/> Turner has said writing melodies is the more difficult part of the songwriting process for him.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner: 'I've forgotten how to write a hit single' |url=https://www.uncut.co.uk/news/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-i-ve-forgotten-how-to-write-a-hit-single-38163/ |access-date=20 January 2020 |work=UNCUT |date=20 December 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Drinks With: Arctic Monkeys {{!}} American Songwriter |url=https://americansongwriter.com/drinks-with-arctic-monkeys/skip-matheny/ |access-date=1 February 2020 |archive-date=15 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191215191016/https://americansongwriter.com/drinks-with-arctic-monkeys/skip-matheny/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Alexis Petridis of ''The Guardian'' believes "Turner's melodic skill sometimes gets overlooked" because he "arrived in the public consciousness words-first."<ref name="Petridis 2016 "/>

He has also said that "there absolutely are images in [his] head when [he] writes", making ''The Car,'' their "most cinematic sounding record".<ref name="Reilly">{{cite web |last=Reilly |first=Nick |date=21 October 2022 |title=Arctic Monkeys 'The Car' review: some of the best songs of their career |url=https://www.rollingstone.co.uk/music/album-reviews/arctic-monkeys-the-car-review-24068/ |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=Rolling Stone UK }}</ref> There's a "grandiose, cinematic quality" that "carries on throughout the album"<ref>{{cite web |last=Chinapen |first=Mark |date=21 October 2022 |title=The Car by Arctic Monkeys {{!}} Album Review |url=https://medium.com/modern-music-analysis/the-car-by-arctic-monkeys-album-review-7edc8dfdd44b |access-date=17 November 2022 |website=Modern Music Analysis }}</ref> "while making things more grander, colorful and cinematic."<ref name="Reilly"/> Talking about [[Mr Schwartz]]'','' he said he imagined the character present while "a production is going on".<ref>{{cite web |title=Arctic Monkeys: 'The Car' and New Beginnings {{!}} Apple Music | website=YouTube | date=20 October 2022 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NGipcn2OU4I |access-date=17 November 2022}}</ref>

===Voice===
In the early years of his career, Turner performed in a strong Sheffield dialect.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sheffield dialect in pop music – Sound and vision blog |url=https://blogs.bl.uk/sound-and-vision/2016/09/sheffield-dialect-in-pop-music.html |access-date=14 January 2020 |work=blogs.bl.uk |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230124211145/https://blogs.bl.uk/sound-and-vision/2016/09/sheffield-dialect-in-pop-music.html |archive-date=24 January 2023 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Simon Armitage remarked: "I can't think of another singer whose regional identity has been so unapologetically and naturally intoned through his singing voice."<ref name=armitage/> By 2018's ''Tranquility Base Hotel'', Alexis Petridis of ''The Guardian'' said: "The Yorkshire dialect that was once his USP is now deployed sparingly, as a jolting effect."<ref name="Petridis Guardian"/> On his accent change, former Arctic Monkeys bassist [[Andy Nicholson]], said: "I don't think he thinks he's [[Elvis Presley|Elvis]], I don't think he's an actor, I think he sometimes probably can live up to the character people are expecting him to be. I mean, he has lived in America, he lived in New York for a long time, and he lived in LA for a long time […] he soaks accents up a lot, 'cause when he started hanging around with [[Miles Kane|Miles]] quite a lot, he started to get a bit of a [[Scouse]] twinge to his accent."<ref>{{cite web |last=Anthony |first=Michael |date=November 2019 |title=[53] Via Hillsborough, Please (Ft. Andy Nicholson) |url=https://open.spotify.com/episode/1Bv6xQ8pncPLeCXjAO2Rbo |access-date=21 May 2023 |website=The Michael Anthony Show}}</ref> One critic said that the tone of Turner's voice has also transformed over time, from a "fidgety whine" to "a worn-in baritone croon".<ref name="iTunes">{{cite news| url = https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/music/live-music-reviews/10298400/Arctic-Monkeys-iTunes-Festival-Roundhouse-review.html| title = Arctic Monkeys, iTunes Festival, Roundhouse, review| work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|date=10 September 2013|access-date=2 May 2018| first=James| last=Lachno}}</ref> About his vocals, Turner told ''[[Rolling Stone|Rolling Stone Germany]]'', "I find the sound and presentation of the voice to be at least as meaningful as the lyrics."<ref name="RollingStoneDE">{{cite magazine|title=Im Herzen Noch Rock-Stars|magazine= Rolling Stone Germany|issue=October 2022|last=Rotifer|first=Robert|date=October 2022|language=de}}</ref> He has noted that the formation of the Last Shadow Puppets was the first time he had "even begun to explore the idea of singing [...] This more classic style of songwriting led to me using my voice in a different way".<ref name="GQ16">{{Cite magazine|last=Lynskey|first=Dorian|title=A Certain Bromance|url=https://archive.org/details/GQ_April_2016_UK/page/218/mode/2up |website=[[GQ|British GQ]]|date=April 2016|pages=216-226|access-date=19 August 2026}}</ref>

===Stage persona===
{{Quote box
 |quote = "When you think about that, and the clothes, I wasn't doing that with ''Suck It and See'' or ''Humbug''. It wasn't grease in the hair. Normally, the record you make encourages a certain style of performance. But thinking about the performer in relation to ''Tranquility'', or even this thing – ''[The Car]'' – I have considered that you can invert that. The performer can influence the music, rather than the other way around."
 |source =— Turner about Performance and [[BRIT Awards]] Speech, 2022<ref name="The GuardianJazz"/>
 |quoted = true
 |bgcolor = #FFFFF1
 |align = right
 |salign = right
 |width = 20em
 |border = 1px
 |fontsize = 95%
}}
In an otherwise positive review of a 2006 show, Richard Cromelin of the ''Los Angeles Times'' noted that Turner seemed "a little spooked by the attention" and hoped he would learn "to reach out more to the audience" in time.<ref name="Welcome to the jungle"/><ref>{{cite news |title=The Arctic Monkeys are a thriving species |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2007-sep-27-wk-monkeys27-story.html |access-date=1 February 2020 |work=Los Angeles Times |date=27 September 2007}}</ref> In 2007, Kitty Empire of ''The Observer'' noted that he was a "reserved" presence on stage: "He chats a bit to about 15 people in the middle of the front rows, and only looks up at the balcony, once, a little apprehensively."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Empire |first1=Kitty |title=Pop: Arctic Monkeys, Astoria, London WC2 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2007/apr/15/popandrock.features1 |access-date=25 January 2020 |work=The Observer |date=14 April 2007}}</ref> Following Arctic Monkeys' headlining appearance at Glastonbury Festival in 2007, Rosie Swash of ''The Guardian'' remarked upon Turner's "steady, wry stage presence": "Arctic Monkeys don't do ad-libbing, they don't do crowd interaction, and they don't do encores."<ref name="swash"/> Simon Price of ''The Independent'' said Turner seemed "to freeze like a rabbit in the spotlights" during a headlining set at Reading Festival in 2009.<ref>{{cite news |title=Reading Festival, Berkshire |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/reviews/reading-festival-berkshire-1782283.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090908201641/http://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/music/reviews/reading-festival-berkshire-1782283.html |archive-date=8 September 2009 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |access-date=8 February 2020 |work=The Independent |date=6 September 2009 }}</ref>

In late 2011, Turner began to change his stage persona and style, most notably changing his hairstyle to a "[[rockabilly]]-inspired [[quiff]]".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Empire |first1=Kitty |title=Arctic Monkeys – review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2011/nov/06/arctic-monkeys-review-manchester |access-date=28 January 2020 |work=The Observer |date=6 November 2011}}</ref> Brian Hiatt of ''Rolling Stone'' noted of his "newfound showmanship" that he "puts his guitar down to strut and dance, drops to his knees for solos when he does play, [and] flirts shamelessly with the female fans".<ref name="hiatt"/> In reviewing a 2013 concert, [[Dorian Lynskey]] of ''The Guardian'' said, "Turner, a shy sort for a frontman, used to seem unnerved by attention and he's coped by adopting a tongue-in-cheek persona that suggests a comic-strip version of a 50s rock star, a [[Blackpool]] [[Buddy Holly]]: all quiff and quips. It's a curious pose, entertaining but alienating in the same way as some of his more arch lyrics. [...] Turner always holds something back, which makes the band more interesting but somewhat distant."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lynskey |first1=Dorian |title=Arctic Monkeys – review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2013/sep/10/arctic-monkeys-itunes-festival-review |access-date=26 January 2020 |work=The Guardian |date=10 September 2013}}</ref><ref name="McLean telegraph">{{cite news |last1=McLean |first1=Craig |title=Arctic Monkeys: 'We've raised the bar as a live band' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/music/rockandpopmusic/10289851/Arctic-Monkeys-Weve-raised-the-bar-as-a-live-band.html |date=31 October 2013|access-date=22 January 2020}}</ref> Ben Beaumont-Thomas of ''The Guardian'' noted in 2018 that Turner ironically "played with the role" of being a rockstar but simultaneously "can't help but be a real rock star".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Beaumont-Thomas |first1=Ben |title=Arctic Monkeys review – cheesy irony dissolves into masterful rock'n'roll |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2018/jun/08/arctic-monkeys-review-royal-albert-hall-london |access-date=29 January 2020 |work=The Guardian |date=8 June 2018}}</ref> While reviewing a 2023 concert, Sian Cain of ''The Guardian'' said, "There are few frontmen touring today who lean into the theatrics of rockstardom as effortlessly as Alex Turner", and felt there were similarities to the performance styles of [[Bob Dylan]] and [[David Byrne]], adding, "He's fond of a [[Camp (style)|campy]] turn and stare".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cain |first1=Sian |title=Arctic Monkeys review – it's Alex Turner's show and we're just living in it|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2023/jan/05/arctic-monkeys-review-its-alex-turners-show-and-were-just-living-in-it|work=The Guardian |date=5 January 2023}}</ref>

Turner has described public speaking as almost being his "worst nightmare"<ref>{{cite news |title=Alex Turner: "Making an Arctic Monkeys album is not an easy alchemy" – Page 2 of 5 |url=https://www.uncut.co.uk/features/alex-turner-making-an-arctic-monkeys-album-is-not-an-easy-alchemy-72724/2/ |access-date=1 February 2020 |work=UNCUT |date=11 May 2018}}</ref> and does not consider himself a "born performer", stating that he "enjoy[s] the studio side of it more than touring".<ref>{{cite news |title=The surprising truth about the Arctic Monkeys |url=https://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/entertainment/music/indie-rock-heroes-arctic-monkeys-sell-out-comeback-gigs-in-australia-with-an-album-made-for-space-rockers/news-story/58bad92f40c797181ad1f5ee2d89d4c9 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181119111229/https://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/entertainment/music/indie-rock-heroes-arctic-monkeys-sell-out-comeback-gigs-in-australia-with-an-album-made-for-space-rockers/news-story/58bad92f40c797181ad1f5ee2d89d4c9 |url-status=live |archive-date=19 November 2018 |access-date=17 January 2020 |work=www.dailytelegraph.com.au |url-access=limited |date=13 November 2018 }}</ref> Although an admitted "control freak" by nature,<ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> he describes being a frontman as an "awkward" and "strange" experience that he does not "take too seriously".<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Arctic Monkeys Conquer America: Alex Turner Q&A |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/pop/arctic-monkeys-conquer-america-alex-turner-qa-6141014/ |access-date=17 January 2020 |magazine=Billboard}}</ref> He said, "I can't go out there and absolutely be myself. The situation is so fundamentally unnatural. [...] It's not a full-on, 'right, get into character' thing ... [sometimes] part of how you actually feel comes out. But I think I always feel weird about that afterwards."<ref name="McLean telegraph"/> Turner felt that being on stage and his songwriting are "a series of intermittent interpretations and impersonations of various artists I admire [...] colliding aggressively and rapidly enough with one another that the origins of the inspiration are largely obscured and from it comes something original."<ref>{{cite web|title=Last Shadow Puppets: 'Pop has become too predictable'|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/music/what-to-listen-to/last-shadow-puppets-pop-has-become-too-predictable/ |work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]|first=Neil|last=McCormick|date=25 June 2016|access-date=10 February 2023}}</ref>

In a 2022 interview with ''The Guardian'', referencing his [[Brit Awards 2014|2014 BRIT Awards]] speech, Turner acknowledged for the first time the use of [[persona]]s, separating himself from what he described as, the "performer" – a fractured reflection of himself– of each album.<ref name="The GuardianJazz">{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2022/sep/30/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-im-comfortable-with-the-idea-that-things-dont-have-to-be-a-pop-song|title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner: 'I'm comfortable with the idea that things don't have to be a pop song|last=Monroe|first=Jazz|date=30 September 2022|website=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=2 October 2022}}</ref> Turner has named one of his former "performers" ''Mr Snarl'', due to the disdainful way in which he sang, Turner said "Sometimes Mr. Snarl shows up", further adding, "It feels completely insane to embody this guy singing to a carpeted room. There I stand, staring at the carpet and wondering how do you sing a damn song like 'Teddy Picker'? But I'm counting on it all making sense again once other people are in the room."<ref name="RollingStoneDE"/>

==Personal life==
Despite significant media attention, Turner rarely discusses his personal life. From 2005 to January 2007, he dated English musician [[Johanna Bennett]] and she was credited as a co-writer on "[[Fluorescent Adolescent]]".<ref name=":1">{{cite news |last=Davis |first=Ben |date=18 May 2010 |title=Thorney girl living the rock star life with Kings of Leon boyfriend – Latest Local News |work=[[Peterborough Evening Telegraph]] |url=http://www.peterboroughtoday.co.uk/news/features/thorney-girl-living-the-rock-star-life-with-kings-of-leon-boyfriend-1-633829 |url-status=dead |access-date=28 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121029115103/http://www.peterboroughtoday.co.uk/news/features/thorney-girl-living-the-rock-star-life-with-kings-of-leon-boyfriend-1-633829 |archive-date=29 October 2012}}</ref><ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" /> He started dating English model and television personality [[Alexa Chung]] in July 2007, and left Sheffield in March 2008, to live with her in London.<ref name="Mojo08">{{Cite news|first=Tom|last=Doyle|title=The Last Shadow Puppets: Best of Both Worlds| website=[[Mojo (magazine)|Mojo]]|date=October 2008|pages=50–54|issue=179}}</ref> In the spring of 2009, they moved to [[Williamsburg, Brooklyn|Williamsburg]], Brooklyn, New York City together, but later relocated to London.<ref name="Barbara theguardian"/> They ended their relationship in July 2011<ref>{{cite web |last=Alexander |first=Ella |title=Alexa Split |url=https://www.vogue.co.uk/article/alexa-chung-and-alex-turner-split |access-date=1 April 2023 |website=British Vogue |date=August 2011 }}</ref> and briefly reunited in the summer of 2014.<ref name=":6">{{cite web |date=1 October 2015 |title=Alexa Chung and Alex Turner Meet in New York – ASOS |url=http://www.asos.com/women/fashion-news/2014_07_7-mon/alexa-chung-alex-turner-meet-in-new-york/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151001005204/http://www.asos.com/women/fashion-news/2014_07_7-mon/alexa-chung-alex-turner-meet-in-new-york/ |archive-date=1 October 2015}}</ref> From late 2011 to early 2014, he dated American actress and model [[Arielle Vandenberg]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Renshaw |first=David |date=13 August 2013 |title=Arctic Monkeys' Alex Turner attends 'Breaking Bad' screening in Los Angeles |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/arctic-monkeys-155-1265024 |access-date=1 April 2023 |website=NME }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Alex Turner Moves On From Alexa Chung With Arielle Vandenberg – Pics |url=https://www.gigwise.com/news/70803/ |access-date=1 April 2023 |website=www.gigwise.com}}</ref> In March 2015, he started dating American model [[Taylor Bagley]]. They lived together in Los Angeles and their relationship came to an end in July 2018.<ref name=":Sunday Times"/><ref>{{cite web |date=3 July 2015 |title=Alex Turner & girlfriend Taylor Bagley still going strong |url=https://www.glamourmagazine.co.uk/article/alex-turner-dating-model-taylor-bagley-relationship-news |access-date=1 April 2023 |website=Glamour UK }}</ref> He began dating French singer-songwriter [[Verlo]] in mid–2018.<ref name="The GuardianJazz" />

He is an avid reader and has cited authors [[Joseph Conrad]], [[Ernest Hemingway]], [[Vladimir Nabokov]],<ref name=":Time Out">{{cite web |last1=Perry |first1=Kevin&nbsp;E.&nbsp;G.|date=20 May 2014 |title=King Of The Swingers |url=https://kevinegperry.com/2014/05/19/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-time-out-interview/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230330152319/https://kevinegperry.com/2014/05/19/arctic-monkeys-alex-turner-time-out-interview/|archive-date=30 March 2023|website=[[Time Out (magazine)|Time Out]]}}</ref> [[David Foster Wallace]], and [[George Saunders]]<ref name="musikexpress.de"/><ref name="ReferenceA"/><ref name="deutschlandfunkkultur.de"/> as some of his favourites. He is also a [[cinephile]], and has shown interest in photography, a hobby he shares with bandmate [[Matt Helders]].<ref name="SPIN">{{cite web |title=Arctic Monkeys Hit a New Gear|url=https://www.spin.com/featured/arctic-monkeys-the-car-cover-story/|last=Appleford|first=Steve|date=18 October 2022|access-date=19 October 2022 |website=[[Spin (magazine)|SPIN]]}}</ref><ref name="youtube.com">{{cite web |title=Entrevista con Alex Turner y Matt Helders {{!}} El regreso de Arctic Monkeys y los secretos de "The Car"|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ag44BzmTOmg |access-date=18 October 2022 |website=Sopitas.com| date=17 October 2022 }}</ref><ref name="Lobb"/> He is a fan of the works of [[Stanley Kubrick]], [[Federico Fellini]], [[Jean-Pierre Melville]], and [[Rainer Werner Fassbinder]] among others.<ref>
